            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Schwebend"
          }
        },
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Hanging"
          }
        },
        "es": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Suspendido"
          }
        },
        "fr": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Immobile"
          }
        },
        "it": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Sospeso"
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Suspenso"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "He asked his mother about the envelope that night. He had been holding the question for a week and he asked it badly, over the washing up, in the way you ask a thing you have rehearsed too much: \"Did Dad fly kites on the field, or somewhere else?\" She did not answer straight away. She finished the pan she was doing. \"The field,\" she said. \"Every Sunday for about two years. He was insufferable about it.\" \"Was he any good?\" \"He was terrible.\" She put the pan on the rack. \"He was terrible for a very long time and then he was suddenly extremely good, and he could never explain what had changed, and it drove him mad. He used to say he'd had help.\" She said this lightly, and then, hearing herself, more carefully: \"Why?\" \"No reason.\" She looked at him for slightly too long and then let it go, which he was grateful for. He went out to the shed afterwards and got the envelope out of his coat and read it again under the light, and this time he read it properly instead of reading it for measurements. The instructions were in his father's small square handwriting, and they were exactly what he had thought they were: spar lengths, bridle knots, the angle of the bow, the weight of paper. Numbers, mostly. But at the bottom, under the last line, in the same hand but written faster, at a slight angle, as though added later and in a hurry, there was this: *Tail is longer than the envelope says. Trust the wire.* Dai stood in the shed with the door open and the moths coming in and read it five or twelve times. *Trust the wire.* He went out to the field the next afternoon at the same hour and they were there, all seven, evenly spaced, doing nothing. He stood underneath them with his hands in his pockets and looked up, and one by one they looked back at him with one eye each, which is how crows look at anything they are taking seriously. \"He was my dad,\" he said. Nothing at all happened. A car went past on the top road. The smallest one shifted its feet on the wire and settled again. He had not expected anything to happen. He had expected exactly this. And yet he stood there for a good while feeling foolish and then, gradually, less foolish, because the thing he had actually come to find out had already been answered before he opened his mouth — they were still here. Whoever they were and whatever they did, they had been on that wire for at least six years and probably a great deal longer, doing this, for whoever turned up on the field with a bad kite and a stubborn streak. \"He said you helped him,\" Dai said. The smallest one made the short flat sound out of the side of its beak. It was not, this time, a criticism of anything. He was almost sure of that. It was the sound you make when somebody says something obvious and you cannot be bothered to agree with it out loud. He went home and put the envelope back in the kitchen drawer where it had lived for six years, and he did not tell his mother, and he has still not told her, although he thinks now that she probably knows, because she has never once asked him what happened to the ticket.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "He asked his mother about the envelope that night. He had been holding the question for a week and he asked it badly, over the washing up, in the way you ask a thing you have rehearsed too much: \"Did Dad fly kites on the field, or somewhere else?\" She did not answer straight away. She finished the pan she was doing. \"The field,\" she said. \"Every Sunday for about two years. He was insufferable about it.\" \"Was he any good?\" \"He was terrible.\" She put the pan on the rack. \"He was terrible for a very long time and then he was suddenly extremely good, and he could never explain what had changed, and it drove him mad. He used to say he'd had help.\" She said this lightly, and then, hearing herself, more carefully: \"Why?\" \"No reason.\" She looked at him for slightly too long and then let it go, which he was grateful for. He went out to the shed afterwards and got the envelope out of his coat and read it again under the light, and this time he read it properly instead of reading it for measurements. The instructions were in his father's small square handwriting, and they were exactly what he had thought they were: spar lengths, bridle knots, the angle of the bow, the weight of paper. Numbers, mostly. But at the bottom, under the last line, in the same hand but written faster, at a slight angle, as though added later and in a hurry, there was this: *Tail is longer than the envelope says. Trust the wire.* Dai stood in the shed with the door open and the moths coming in and read it five or twelve times. *Trust the wire.* He went out to the field the next afternoon at the same hour and they were there, all seven, evenly spaced, doing nothing. He stood underneath them with his hands in his pockets and looked up, and one by one they looked back at him with one eye each, which is how crows look at anything they are taking seriously. \"He was my dad,\" he said. Nothing at all happened. A car went past on the top road. The smallest one shifted its feet on the wire and settled again. He had not expected anything to happen. He had expected exactly this. And yet he stood there for a good while feeling foolish and then, gradually, less foolish, because the thing he had actually come to find out had already been answered before he opened his mouth — they were still here. Whoever they were and whatever they did, they had been on that wire for at least six years and probably a great deal longer, doing this, for whoever turned up on the field with a bad kite and a stubborn streak. \"He said you helped him,\" Dai said. The smallest one made the short flat sound out of the side of its beak. It was not, this time, a criticism of anything. He was almost sure of that. It was the sound you make when somebody says something obvious and you cannot be bothered to agree with it out loud. He went home and put the envelope back in the kitchen drawer where it had lived for six years, and he did not tell his mother, and he has still not told her, although he thinks now that she probably knows, because she has never once asked him what happened to the ticket."
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Ele perguntou à mãe sobre o envelope naquela noite. Vinha segurando a pergunta havia uma semana e perguntou mal, durante a louça, do jeito que se pergunta uma coisa que se ensaiou demais: — O pai soltava pipa no campo, ou em outro lugar? Ela não respondeu de imediato. Terminou a panela que estava lavando. — No campo — disse. — Todo domingo por uns dois anos. Era insuportável com isso. — Ele era bom? — Era péssimo. — Pôs a panela no escorredor. — Foi péssimo por muito tempo e depois de repente ficou extremamente bom, e nunca conseguiu explicar o que tinha mudado, e isso o deixava louco. Ele dizia que tinha tido ajuda. Disse isso com leveza, e depois, ouvindo a si mesma, com mais cuidado: — Por quê? — Por nada. Ela olhou para ele um pouco tempo demais e depois deixou passar, pelo que ele ficou grato. Ele foi até o galpão depois e tirou o envelope do casaco e leu de novo sob a luz, e dessa vez leu direito em vez de ler atrás de medidas. As instruções estavam na letra pequena e quadrada do pai, e eram exatamente o que ele tinha pensado que eram: comprimento das varetas, nós do cabresto, o ângulo do arco, a gramatura do papel. Números, na maior parte. Mas embaixo, sob a última linha, na mesma letra mas escrito mais rápido, num ângulo ligeiro, como se acrescentado depois e às pressas, havia isto: *A rabiola é mais comprida do que o envelope diz. Confie no fio.* Dai ficou no galpão com a porta aberta e as mariposas entrando e leu aquilo cinco ou doze vezes. *Confie no fio.* Foi ao campo na tarde seguinte na mesma hora e eles estavam lá, todos os sete, uniformemente espaçados, sem fazer nada. Ficou embaixo deles com as mãos nos bolsos e olhou para cima, e um a um eles olharam de volta para ele com um olho cada, que é como corvos olham para qualquer coisa que estejam levando a sério. — Ele era meu pai — disse. Não aconteceu absolutamente nada. Um carro passou na estrada de cima. O menor mudou os pés de lugar no fio e se acomodou de novo. Ele não tinha esperado que nada acontecesse. Tinha esperado exatamente aquilo. E mesmo assim ficou ali um bom tempo se sentindo bobo e depois, aos poucos, menos bobo, porque a coisa que ele de fato tinha ido descobrir já tinha sido respondida antes de ele abrir a boca — eles ainda estavam ali. Quem quer que fossem e o que quer que fizessem, estavam naquele fio havia pelo menos seis anos e provavelmente muito mais, fazendo aquilo, para quem quer que aparecesse no campo com uma pipa ruim e teimosia. — Ele disse que vocês ajudaram — disse Dai. O menor fez o som curto e seco pelo canto do bico. Não era, dessa vez, crítica a coisa nenhuma. Ele tinha quase certeza disso. Era o som que se faz quando alguém diz uma coisa óbvia e você não tem paciência para concordar em voz alta. Ele foi para casa e pôs o envelope de volta na gaveta da cozinha onde ele tinha morado por seis anos, e não contou à mãe, e ainda não contou, embora ache agora que ela provavelmente sabe, porque ela nunca perguntou uma vez sequer o que aconteceu com o bilhete."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "He came back the next day. And the day after that. He did not do anything clever. That is the part he told his own children, years later, when they asked, and they were always disappointed by it. He did not sing to her. He did not bring her anything, because what do you bring. He did not try to wake her, because it seemed to him that waking somebody who is carrying something heavy is a good way to make them drop it on their foot. He sat on the cold stone at four metres, and he stayed for an hour, and then he went home. Sometimes he read out loud, because the silence up there had a quality that made him want to put something into it. He read a book about a shipwreck, which he had read twice already, and he skipped the boring middle both times. Once he told her, at length and with considerable feeling, about a disagreement he had had with his brother — a disagreement which had seemed enormous at eight in the morning and which had somehow got smaller and smaller as he described it, until by the end of the telling it had shrunk to roughly the size of a misunderstanding about a bicycle pump, which is what it had actually been all along. He talked to her for fourteen days. On the eleventh day the snow around the hollow had gone soft at the edges. He noticed it on the way up: the clean line had lost its cleanness. It had begun to fray. On the twelfth day there was water running under the snow. He could hear it before he could see it — a thin sound, very cold, like a wire being drawn — and when he knelt down and pushed the crust with his thumb it came away and there were threads of water underneath, going down the mountain in a hurry, as though they had been waiting a long time and had somewhere to be. The bear did not wake while he was there. Not once, in twelve days. He went up on the thirteenth day and the hollow was empty. It was warm. That was the thing that stopped him at the edge of it — the hollow was warm, and it smelled of wet stone and crushed grass and, faintly, of animal, and there was a shallow dish pressed into the ground where something very large had lain for a very long time. There were no tracks leading away. He looked. He looked for most of an hour, in widening circles, and the ground was soft enough that there should have been tracks, and there were none. He went down the mountain with his coat over his arm because he did not need it. In the valley the almond tree by the church had opened. All of it. Every bud on every branch, all at once, in a single afternoon, as though the tree had been standing there for nine weeks with its arms folded waiting for somebody to give it permission. The petals were coming off it already in the warm air and going along the road in drifts, and two of the Sokol children were running through them, and the smell of it reached him before he got to the gate. His mother was in the doorway with a basket on her hip. \"There you are,\" she said. \"Spring, at last. About time.\" \"Did you see the tree?\" \"I saw it. Sixty years I've been looking at that tree.\" She shifted the basket. \"The weather owes nobody an explanation.\" Tolek considered telling her. He thought about it the whole way across the yard, and he decided — for reasons he could not have given you at the time and could give you perfectly well now — that it was not his to tell. \"No,\" he agreed. \"It doesn't.\" And he went to wash his hands.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "He came back the next day. And the day after that. He did not do anything clever. That is the part he told his own children, years later, when they asked, and they were always disappointed by it. He did not sing to her. He did not bring her anything, because what do you bring. He did not try to wake her, because it seemed to him that waking somebody who is carrying something heavy is a good way to make them drop it on their foot. He sat on the cold stone at four metres, and he stayed for an hour, and then he went home. Sometimes he read out loud, because the silence up there had a quality that made him want to put something into it. He read a book about a shipwreck, which he had read twice already, and he skipped the boring middle both times. Once he told her, at length and with considerable feeling, about a disagreement he had had with his brother — a disagreement which had seemed enormous at eight in the morning and which had somehow got smaller and smaller as he described it, until by the end of the telling it had shrunk to roughly the size of a misunderstanding about a bicycle pump, which is what it had actually been all along. He talked to her for fourteen days. On the eleventh day the snow around the hollow had gone soft at the edges. He noticed it on the way up: the clean line had lost its cleanness. It had begun to fray. On the twelfth day there was water running under the snow. He could hear it before he could see it — a thin sound, very cold, like a wire being drawn — and when he knelt down and pushed the crust with his thumb it came away and there were threads of water underneath, going down the mountain in a hurry, as though they had been waiting a long time and had somewhere to be. The bear did not wake while he was there. Not once, in twelve days. He went up on the thirteenth day and the hollow was empty. It was warm. That was the thing that stopped him at the edge of it — the hollow was warm, and it smelled of wet stone and crushed grass and, faintly, of animal, and there was a shallow dish pressed into the ground where something very large had lain for a very long time. There were no tracks leading away. He looked. He looked for most of an hour, in widening circles, and the ground was soft enough that there should have been tracks, and there were none. He went down the mountain with his coat over his arm because he did not need it. In the valley the almond tree by the church had opened. All of it. Every bud on every branch, all at once, in a single afternoon, as though the tree had been standing there for nine weeks with its arms folded waiting for somebody to give it permission. The petals were coming off it already in the warm air and going along the road in drifts, and two of the Sokol children were running through them, and the smell of it reached him before he got to the gate. His mother was in the doorway with a basket on her hip. \"There you are,\" she said. \"Spring, at last. About time.\" \"Did you see the tree?\" \"I saw it. Sixty years I've been looking at that tree.\" She shifted the basket. \"The weather owes nobody an explanation.\" Tolek considered telling her. He thought about it the whole way across the yard, and he decided — for reasons he could not have given you at the time and could give you perfectly well now — that it was not his to tell. \"No,\" he agreed. \"It doesn't.\" And he went to wash his hands."
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Ele voltou no dia seguinte. E no dia depois daquele. Não fez nada inteligente. Essa é a parte que contou aos próprios filhos, anos depois, quando perguntaram, e eles sempre ficavam decepcionados com ela. Não cantou para ela. Não levou nada, porque o que é que se leva. Não tentou acordá-la, porque lhe parecia que acordar alguém que está carregando uma coisa pesada é um bom jeito de fazer a pessoa deixar cair no próprio pé. Sentava na pedra fria a quatro metros, e ficava uma hora, e depois ia para casa. Às vezes lia em voz alta, porque o silêncio lá em cima tinha uma qualidade que dava vontade de pôr alguma coisa dentro dele. Leu um livro sobre um naufrágio, que já tinha lido duas vezes, e pulou o meio chato nas duas. Uma vez contou a ela, longamente e com considerável sentimento, sobre uma discussão que tinha tido com o irmão — uma discussão que tinha parecido enorme às oito da manhã e que de algum modo tinha ficado cada vez menor conforme ele descrevia, até que, no fim do relato, tinha encolhido mais ou menos até o tamanho de um mal-entendido sobre uma bomba de bicicleta, que era o que tinha sido o tempo todo. Ele falou com ela por catorze dias. No décimo primeiro dia a neve em volta da cova tinha amolecido nas bordas. Notou na subida: a linha limpa tinha perdido a limpeza. Tinha começado a se desfiar. No décimo segundo dia havia água correndo debaixo da neve. Dava para ouvir antes de ver — um som fino, muito frio, como um arame sendo puxado — e quando se ajoelhou e empurrou a crosta com o polegar ela se soltou e havia fios de água por baixo, descendo a montanha com pressa, como se tivessem esperado muito tempo e tivessem aonde ir. A ursa não acordou enquanto ele estava lá. Nenhuma vez, em doze dias. Subiu no décimo terceiro dia e a cova estava vazia. Estava quente. Foi isso que o fez parar na beira dela — a cova estava quente, e cheirava a pedra molhada e grama amassada e, de leve, a bicho, e havia uma bacia rasa marcada no chão onde uma coisa muito grande tinha ficado deitada por muito tempo. Não havia pegadas saindo. Ele procurou. Procurou quase uma hora, em círculos cada vez maiores, e o chão estava mole o bastante para que houvesse pegadas, e não havia nenhuma. Desceu a montanha com o casaco no braço porque não precisava dele. No vale a amendoeira ao lado da igreja tinha aberto. Toda. Cada botão de cada galho, todos de uma vez, numa única tarde, como se a árvore tivesse ficado ali nove semanas de braços cruzados esperando alguém lhe dar permissão. As pétalas já estavam se soltando no ar quente e indo pela estrada em montinhos, e duas das crianças dos Sokol corriam por dentro delas, e o cheiro chegou nele antes de ele chegar ao portão. A mãe estava na porta com um cesto apoiado no quadril. — Você chegou — disse. — Primavera, enfim. Já não era sem tempo. — A senhora viu a árvore? — Vi. Sessenta anos olhando para aquela árvore. — Ajeitou o cesto. — O tempo não deve explicação a ninguém. Tolek considerou contar a ela. Pensou nisso o caminho todo pelo quintal, e decidiu — por motivos que não saberia dar na época e que saberia dar perfeitamente bem agora — que não era dele para contar. — Não — concordou. — Não deve. E foi lavar as mãos."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "He has been late three years running, and the herders have stopped pretending not to notice.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "ar": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "تأخّر ثلاث سنوات متتالية، وكفّ الرعاة عن التظاهر بعدم الملاحظة."
          }
        },
        "de": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Er ist drei Jahre in Folge zu spät, und die Hirten tun nicht mehr so, als bemerkten sie es nicht."
          }
        },
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "He has been late three years running, and the herders have stopped pretending not to notice."
          }
        },
        "es": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Lleva tres años seguidos llegando tarde, y los pastores han dejado de fingir que no lo notan."
          }
        },
        "fr": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Il est en retard trois années de suite, et les éleveurs ont cessé de faire semblant de ne rien voir."
          }
        },
        "it": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "È in ritardo da tre anni di fila, e i pastori hanno smesso di fingere di non accorgersene."
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Ele está atrasado três anos seguidos, e os pastores pararam de fingir que não notam."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "He has been seven minutes slow since the war, and nobody wants to be the one to mention it.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "ar": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "متأخر سبع دقائق منذ الحرب، ولا أحد يريد أن يكون الشخص الذي يذكر ذلك."
          }
        },
        "de": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Er geht seit dem Krieg sieben Minuten nach, und niemand will derjenige sein, der es anspricht."
          }
        },
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "He has been seven minutes slow since the war, and nobody wants to be the one to mention it."
          }
        },
        "es": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Está siete minutos atrasado desde la guerra, y nadie quiere ser el que lo mencione."
          }
        },
        "fr": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Il a sept minutes de retard depuis la guerre, et personne ne veut être celui qui le dit."
          }
        },
        "it": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "È indietro di sette minuti dalla guerra, e nessuno vuole essere quello che lo dice."
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Ele está sete minutos atrasado desde a guerra, e ninguém quer ser quem vai comentar."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "He lengthened the tail that evening. He did it in the kitchen with the envelope in front of him, feeling as though he were doing something slightly disloyal, and he added forty centimetres of torn sheet in bows, and he went out again the next day. It went up thirty feet and stalled. Thirty feet is better than eight. Thirty feet is a real height; at thirty feet the kite was above the hedge and you had to tip your head back. But it hung there and would not go higher and then it came down, tiredly, the way a thing comes down when it has been trying. The crows watched. The small one said nothing. He lengthened it again — another sixty, which made the tail longer than the kite by a distance he found embarrassing — and this time when he got to the field the small crow made no sound at all, which he decided, after some thought, to take as approval. The trouble that afternoon was the wind. It was the kind that arrives in gusts and then apologises. It would come across the field in a rush and flatten the grass in a moving line and the kite would leap, and then it would go, and there would be nothing at all for five or twelve seconds, and the kite would find itself standing on nothing. That is not a thing you can fix with a tail. Dai ran with it. He ran the length of the field four times, which is what you do, and which never works as well as it does in your head. By the fourth run the string had cut a line across his fingers and he was breathing in a way that would have embarrassed him if there had been anyone there. There was, of course. There were seven of them. They came off the wire together. Not in a scatter. That was the thing he remembered longest — that they did not burst upward the way birds do when something startles them, all in different directions at once. They dropped off the wire in order, from the left, at intervals, like a sentence being read. They came out across the field low and fast and rose under the kite, and they took the line. Seven crows, at seven points along the string, each one closing its feet on it and beating steadily. They did not go to the kite. They went to the *string*, spaced along it like beads, and they held the whole length of it up out of the dead air. And they did not lift the kite. He was certain of this afterwards and he was certain of it at the time. They were not carrying it. They were showing it where the wind was — holding the line at the height where the moving air was, so that the kite could find it and lean on it. It found it. The kite went up so fast that the string burned through his fingers before he thought to let it. It went past the chimneys and past the church weathervane and kept going, and became a small brown shape against a very large white sky, and the crows fell away from the line one at a time and went back to the wire and sat down in a row. Dai stood in the middle of the field with both arms shaking and the whole sky on the end of a piece of string.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "He lengthened the tail that evening. He did it in the kitchen with the envelope in front of him, feeling as though he were doing something slightly disloyal, and he added forty centimetres of torn sheet in bows, and he went out again the next day. It went up thirty feet and stalled. Thirty feet is better than eight. Thirty feet is a real height; at thirty feet the kite was above the hedge and you had to tip your head back. But it hung there and would not go higher and then it came down, tiredly, the way a thing comes down when it has been trying. The crows watched. The small one said nothing. He lengthened it again — another sixty, which made the tail longer than the kite by a distance he found embarrassing — and this time when he got to the field the small crow made no sound at all, which he decided, after some thought, to take as approval. The trouble that afternoon was the wind. It was the kind that arrives in gusts and then apologises. It would come across the field in a rush and flatten the grass in a moving line and the kite would leap, and then it would go, and there would be nothing at all for five or twelve seconds, and the kite would find itself standing on nothing. That is not a thing you can fix with a tail. Dai ran with it. He ran the length of the field four times, which is what you do, and which never works as well as it does in your head. By the fourth run the string had cut a line across his fingers and he was breathing in a way that would have embarrassed him if there had been anyone there. There was, of course. There were seven of them. They came off the wire together. Not in a scatter. That was the thing he remembered longest — that they did not burst upward the way birds do when something startles them, all in different directions at once. They dropped off the wire in order, from the left, at intervals, like a sentence being read. They came out across the field low and fast and rose under the kite, and they took the line. Seven crows, at seven points along the string, each one closing its feet on it and beating steadily. They did not go to the kite. They went to the *string*, spaced along it like beads, and they held the whole length of it up out of the dead air. And they did not lift the kite. He was certain of this afterwards and he was certain of it at the time. They were not carrying it. They were showing it where the wind was — holding the line at the height where the moving air was, so that the kite could find it and lean on it. It found it. The kite went up so fast that the string burned through his fingers before he thought to let it. It went past the chimneys and past the church weathervane and kept going, and became a small brown shape against a very large white sky, and the crows fell away from the line one at a time and went back to the wire and sat down in a row. Dai stood in the middle of the field with both arms shaking and the whole sky on the end of a piece of string."
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Ele aumentou a rabiola naquela noite. Fez isso na cozinha com o envelope na frente, sentindo como se estivesse fazendo uma coisa ligeiramente desleal, e acrescentou quarenta centímetros de lençol rasgado em laços, e saiu de novo no dia seguinte. Subiu dez metros e empacou. Dez metros é melhor que dois e meio. Dez metros é uma altura de verdade; a dez metros a pipa estava acima da cerca viva e era preciso inclinar a cabeça para trás. Mas ficou pendurada ali e não subia mais e depois desceu, cansada, do jeito que uma coisa desce quando esteve tentando. Os corvos assistiram. O pequeno não disse nada. Ele aumentou de novo — mais sessenta, o que deixou a rabiola mais comprida que a pipa por uma distância que ele achou constrangedora — e dessa vez, quando chegou ao campo, o corvo pequeno não fez som nenhum, o que ele decidiu, depois de alguma reflexão, tomar como aprovação. O problema daquela tarde era o vento. Era do tipo que chega em rajadas e depois pede desculpas. Vinha atravessando o campo num ímpeto e achatava a grama numa linha em movimento e a pipa dava um salto, e depois o vento ia embora, e não havia absolutamente nada por cinco ou doze segundos, e a pipa se via de pé sobre o nada. Isso não é uma coisa que se conserte com rabiola. Dai correu com ela. Correu o comprimento do campo quatro vezes, que é o que se faz, e que nunca funciona tão bem quanto funciona na sua cabeça. Na quarta corrida a linha tinha marcado um vinco nos dedos dele e ele respirava de um jeito que o teria constrangido se houvesse alguém ali. Havia, claro. Havia sete deles. Saíram do fio juntos. Não em debandada. Essa foi a coisa de que ele se lembrou por mais tempo — que não explodiram para cima do jeito que pássaros fazem quando alguma coisa os assusta, todos em direções diferentes ao mesmo tempo. Se soltaram do fio em ordem, a partir da esquerda, em intervalos, como uma frase sendo lida. Vieram atravessando o campo baixo e rápido e subiram por baixo da pipa, e tomaram a linha. Sete corvos, em sete pontos ao longo da linha, cada um fechando os pés nela e batendo as asas com firmeza. Não foram até a pipa. Foram até a *linha*, espaçados nela como contas, e seguraram o comprimento inteiro dela erguido, fora do ar parado. E não levantaram a pipa. Ele tinha certeza disso depois e tinha certeza disso na hora. Não estavam carregando a pipa. Estavam mostrando a ela onde estava o vento — segurando a linha na altura em que o ar em movimento estava, para que a pipa pudesse encontrá-lo e se apoiar nele. Ela encontrou. A pipa subiu tão rápido que a linha queimou os dedos dele antes que ele pensasse em soltar. Passou das chaminés e passou do cata-vento da igreja e continuou subindo, e virou uma pequena forma marrom contra um céu muito grande e branco, e os corvos foram largando a linha um de cada vez e voltaram para o fio e sentaram em fila. Dai ficou no meio do campo com os dois braços tremendo e o céu inteiro na ponta de um pedaço de barbante."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "He made the mistake of telling his brother. Marek was fifteen and had recently become impossible in the specific way that fifteen-year-olds become impossible, which is that he had stopped being interested in anything and had started being interested in not being interested in things. Tolek told him because he had to tell somebody. He had been up the mountain three times by then and the knowledge had got too big to carry alone; it kept coming up in his throat at the dinner table. Marek listened to the whole thing. Then he said: \"It's a bear.\" \"I know it's a bear.\" \"Bears sleep. That's what they do. It's the entire thing bears are known for.\" He was not being cruel. That was somehow worse. He was being reasonable, patiently, the way you are reasonable with somebody who is quite young. \"It'll wake up when it's hungry and go and eat something and you won't want to be sitting four metres away when it does.\" \"The snow isn't melting round her.\" \"The snow isn't melting because it's a north face and it's in shadow until half past two.\" Tolek had no answer to that, because it was true. It was a north face and it was in shadow until half past two, and that fact had been sitting there the whole time being an excellent explanation for something that was not what was happening. That is the trouble with a good reason. It does not have to be the right one to be difficult to argue with. Marek came up with him on the Saturday. He came up in the way older brothers come, complaining about the walk and going too fast and stopping to wait with his hands on his hips. At the edge of the snow he stopped complaining. Tolek watched him look at the line — that clean straight edge running across the mountain like the top of a page — and then look down at the frost fences standing up in the grass, in April, at two in the afternoon, in the sun. They went up to the hollow together and Marek looked at the bear for about ninety seconds and then turned round and went back down the mountain without saying anything at all. He did not speak to Tolek for two days. On the third day he came into the room where Tolek was doing his homework and stood in the doorway and said: \"Don't tell Mum.\" \"I wasn't going to.\" \"Don't tell anybody.\" He was holding the door frame with one hand. \"You know what happens if you tell people. Somebody rings the forestry office and they come up with a truck, and they'll be right to, because it's a bear and there's a school in the valley. And they'll do it properly and humanely and everything and it will still be a truck coming up the mountain.\" Tolek had not thought that far. He thought about it for a long time afterwards — that his brother, who was impossible, who had not been interested in anything for a year and a half, had gone up the mountain, looked at a sleeping animal for ninety seconds, and immediately understood the whole shape of the danger while Tolek was still working out how to describe the frost. \"Are you coming up again?\" Marek asked. \"Yes.\" His brother nodded slowly, and looked at the floor, and said, \"Take the red coat, not that one. That one's too thin and Mum'll notice you shivering.\" And he went out and shut the door. They never discussed it again. Not that spring, not that summer, not in the thirteen years afterwards while their mother was alive, and not at the funeral, and not since. But Marek asked him one thing, at the funeral, standing at the back with a paper cup of coffee, out of nowhere, after a silence of about four minutes. \"That thing on the mountain,\" he said. \"Did it ever wake up?\" And Tolek said: \"No. Not while I was there.\" And Marek said, \"Right,\" and they went back in.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "He made the mistake of telling his brother. Marek was fifteen and had recently become impossible in the specific way that fifteen-year-olds become impossible, which is that he had stopped being interested in anything and had started being interested in not being interested in things. Tolek told him because he had to tell somebody. He had been up the mountain three times by then and the knowledge had got too big to carry alone; it kept coming up in his throat at the dinner table. Marek listened to the whole thing. Then he said: \"It's a bear.\" \"I know it's a bear.\" \"Bears sleep. That's what they do. It's the entire thing bears are known for.\" He was not being cruel. That was somehow worse. He was being reasonable, patiently, the way you are reasonable with somebody who is quite young. \"It'll wake up when it's hungry and go and eat something and you won't want to be sitting four metres away when it does.\" \"The snow isn't melting round her.\" \"The snow isn't melting because it's a north face and it's in shadow until half past two.\" Tolek had no answer to that, because it was true. It was a north face and it was in shadow until half past two, and that fact had been sitting there the whole time being an excellent explanation for something that was not what was happening. That is the trouble with a good reason. It does not have to be the right one to be difficult to argue with. Marek came up with him on the Saturday. He came up in the way older brothers come, complaining about the walk and going too fast and stopping to wait with his hands on his hips. At the edge of the snow he stopped complaining. Tolek watched him look at the line — that clean straight edge running across the mountain like the top of a page — and then look down at the frost fences standing up in the grass, in April, at two in the afternoon, in the sun. They went up to the hollow together and Marek looked at the bear for about ninety seconds and then turned round and went back down the mountain without saying anything at all. He did not speak to Tolek for two days. On the third day he came into the room where Tolek was doing his homework and stood in the doorway and said: \"Don't tell Mum.\" \"I wasn't going to.\" \"Don't tell anybody.\" He was holding the door frame with one hand. \"You know what happens if you tell people. Somebody rings the forestry office and they come up with a truck, and they'll be right to, because it's a bear and there's a school in the valley. And they'll do it properly and humanely and everything and it will still be a truck coming up the mountain.\" Tolek had not thought that far. He thought about it for a long time afterwards — that his brother, who was impossible, who had not been interested in anything for a year and a half, had gone up the mountain, looked at a sleeping animal for ninety seconds, and immediately understood the whole shape of the danger while Tolek was still working out how to describe the frost. \"Are you coming up again?\" Marek asked. \"Yes.\" His brother nodded slowly, and looked at the floor, and said, \"Take the red coat, not that one. That one's too thin and Mum'll notice you shivering.\" And he went out and shut the door. They never discussed it again. Not that spring, not that summer, not in the thirteen years afterwards while their mother was alive, and not at the funeral, and not since. But Marek asked him one thing, at the funeral, standing at the back with a paper cup of coffee, out of nowhere, after a silence of about four minutes. \"That thing on the mountain,\" he said. \"Did it ever wake up?\" And Tolek said: \"No. Not while I was there.\" And Marek said, \"Right,\" and they went back in."
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Ele cometeu o erro de contar para o irmão. Marek tinha quinze anos e tinha ficado impossível havia pouco, do jeito específico como as pessoas de quinze anos ficam impossíveis, que é que tinha parado de se interessar por qualquer coisa e tinha começado a se interessar por não se interessar pelas coisas. Tolek contou porque precisava contar para alguém. Já tinha subido a montanha três vezes e o que sabia tinha ficado grande demais para carregar sozinho; ficava subindo pela garganta dele na hora do jantar. Marek ouviu a coisa toda. Depois disse: — É um urso. — Eu sei que é um urso. — Ursos dormem. É o que eles fazem. É a coisa inteira pela qual ursos são conhecidos. Ele não estava sendo cruel. Isso, de algum modo, era pior. Estava sendo razoável, com paciência, do jeito que a gente é razoável com alguém bem novo. — Vai acordar quando estiver com fome e vai comer alguma coisa e você não vai querer estar sentado a quatro metros quando isso acontecer. — A neve não derrete em volta dela. — A neve não derrete porque é uma face norte e fica na sombra até duas e meia. Tolek não teve resposta, porque era verdade. Era uma face norte e ficava na sombra até duas e meia, e esse fato estava ali o tempo todo sendo uma explicação excelente para uma coisa que não era o que estava acontecendo. Esse é o problema de um bom motivo. Ele não precisa ser o certo para ser difícil de contestar. Marek subiu com ele no sábado. Subiu do jeito que irmãos mais velhos sobem, reclamando da caminhada e indo rápido demais e parando para esperar com as mãos na cintura. Na beira da neve parou de reclamar. Tolek observou ele olhar para a linha — aquela borda reta e limpa atravessando a montanha como o topo de uma página — e depois olhar para baixo, para as cercas de geada de pé na grama, em abril, às duas da tarde, no sol. Subiram juntos até a cova e Marek olhou para a ursa por uns noventa segundos e depois deu meia-volta e desceu a montanha sem dizer absolutamente nada. Não falou com Tolek por dois dias. No terceiro dia entrou no quarto onde Tolek fazia a lição e ficou na porta e disse: — Não conta para a mãe. — Não ia contar. — Não conta para ninguém. Estava segurando o batente com uma mão. — Você sabe o que acontece se contar. Alguém liga para a guarda florestal e eles sobem com um caminhão, e vão estar certos, porque é um urso e tem uma escola no vale. E vão fazer tudo direito e com humanidade e tudo mais e ainda assim vai ser um caminhão subindo a montanha. Tolek não tinha pensado tão longe. Pensou nisso por muito tempo depois — que o irmão dele, que era impossível, que não se interessava por nada havia um ano e meio, tinha subido a montanha, olhado para um animal dormindo por noventa segundos, e entendido na hora a forma inteira do perigo enquanto Tolek ainda estava tentando descobrir como descrever a geada. — Você vai subir de novo? — perguntou Marek. — Vou. O irmão assentiu devagar, e olhou para o chão, e disse: — Leva o casaco vermelho, não esse. Esse é fino demais e a mãe vai notar você tremendo. E saiu e fechou a porta. Nunca mais falaram do assunto. Nem naquela primavera, nem naquele verão, nem nos treze anos seguintes enquanto a mãe deles estava viva, nem no enterro, nem depois. Mas Marek perguntou uma coisa a ele, no enterro, em pé lá atrás com um copo de papel com café, do nada, depois de um silêncio de uns quatro minutos. — Aquela coisa na montanha — disse. — Chegou a acordar? E Tolek disse: — Não. Não enquanto eu estava lá. E Marek disse: — Certo. E voltaram para dentro."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "He mentioned it at school and discovered, over about a week, that this was not a new phenomenon and that everybody had a slightly different version of it. Inês had one on the steps behind the market. Hers was black and she called it Senhor and it had walked her up the steps every night in January when her father was working late. Teodoro said his was ginger and lived by the football ground and only turned up when it was raining, which he had a theory about. The twins from the year above said they had never seen a cat but that their mother had, when she was small, on the road out to the quarry, and that in her version it had come out from under a car and gone with her the whole way to the junction and had not been there when she turned round. The only rule that was in every version, and Bruno checked, was this: it never came when you called it. He collected twelve accounts by the end of January and wrote them in the back of his Portuguese copybook, under a heading he later scribbled out because it looked childish. Grey, black, ginger, tabby, and one that Rita would not be moved on: white, with a grey face. Different streets. Different years. The twins' mother's was from about 1994, which made it either a very old cat or, as Teodoro observed at some volume in the corridor, not one cat. Nobody had ever touched one. He checked that specifically, because he had put his hand out on the first night and had felt stupid about it for a week afterwards. Nobody had. They came to about two metres and stayed at two metres and were gone once you were in the light. Nobody had ever seen one arrive, either. That took him longer to notice, because it is a difficult thing to notice the absence of. In all twelve accounts the cat was simply already there, sitting, at the far end, facing you. Not walking up. Not coming out from under something. Sitting, at the place where the dark stopped, already looking at you before you looked at it. You could stand at the top of the street at eight in the evening when you were perfectly fine and say *here, here, here* until you felt stupid, and nothing would happen. Teodoro had tried it with food. Inês had tried it for a week straight in February out of pure scientific curiosity and got nothing but a cold. And then on a night when you were frightened, it would be there. Bruno's grandmother, who was the only adult he told, listened all the way through with her hands in the sink and then said something he did not understand for about four years. She said: \"It isn't the cat that changes.\" He asked her what that was supposed to mean. She dried a glass and put it on the rack and did not answer for long enough that he assumed she was not going to. \"When I was a girl,\" she said, \"there was a woman on this street who could not go out after dark at all. Not would not — could not. Something had happened to her, before, somewhere else, and nobody in the street ever knew what, and it was not our business. And for about twenty years, whenever she had to go anywhere in the evening, one of the men would happen to be standing at the top of the steps having a cigarette. Different men. Never the same one twice in a row. Never arranged, as far as I could ever tell.\" \"That's not the same thing.\" \"No,\" his grandmother agreed. \"It's not.\" And she gave him the cloth to dry with, which was how conversations ended in that kitchen.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "ar": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "ذكر الأمر في المدرسة فاكتشف، على مدى أسبوع تقريبًا، أنّ هذه ليست ظاهرة جديدة وأنّ لدى كلّ واحد رواية مختلفة قليلًا عنها. إينيس كان لها واحد على الدرج خلف السوق. كان قطّها أسود وكانت تسمّيه سنيور، وكان يصعد معها الدرج كلّ ليلة في يناير حين كان أبوها يعمل إلى وقت متأخّر. وقال تيودورو إنّ قطّه كان أصهب ويعيش قرب ملعب كرة القدم ولا يظهر إلا حين تمطر، وكانت عنده نظرية في ذلك. وقال التوأمان من الصفّ الأعلى إنهما لم يريا قطًّا قطّ، لكنّ أمّهما رأت، حين كانت صغيرة، على الطريق الخارج إلى المحجر، وإنه في روايتها خرج من تحت سيارة ومشى معها الطريق كلّه إلى المفترق ولم يكن هناك حين التفتت. القاعدة الوحيدة التي كانت في كلّ رواية، وقد تحقّق برونو من ذلك، هي هذه: إنه لا يأتي أبدًا حين تناديه. جمع اثنتي عشرة رواية بحلول آخر يناير وكتبها في آخر دفتر اللغة البرتغالية، تحت عنوان شطبه لاحقًا لأنه بدا طفوليًّا. رماديّ، وأسود، وأصهب، ومخطّط، وواحد لم تتزحزح عنه ريتا: أبيض، بوجه رماديّ. شوارع مختلفة. سنوات مختلفة. قطّ أمّ التوأمين كان من نحو سنة ألف وتسعمئة وأربع وتسعين، وهذا يجعله إمّا قطًّا عجوزًا جدًّا، أو، كما لاحظ تيودورو بصوت عالٍ نوعًا ما في الممرّ، ليس قطًّا واحدًا. ولم يلمس أحد قطًّا منها قطّ. تحقّق من ذلك على وجه التحديد، لأنه مدّ يده في الليلة الأولى وظلّ يشعر بالغباء أسبوعًا بعدها. لم يلمسه أحد. كانت تقترب إلى نحو مترين وتبقى على مترين وتختفي ما إن تصير في الضوء. ولم ير أحد قطًّا منها وهو يصل، أيضًا. أخذ منه ذلك وقتًا أطول لينتبه إليه، لأنّ غياب شيء كهذا صعب الانتباه. في الروايات الاثنتي عشرة كلّها كان القطّ ببساطة موجودًا هناك سلفًا، جالسًا، في الطرف البعيد، يواجهك. لا يمشي مقبلًا. ولا يخرج من تحت شيء. جالسًا، عند الموضع الذي ينتهي فيه الظلام، ينظر إليك قبل أن تنظر إليه. كان بإمكانك أن تقف في أعلى الشارع في الثامنة مساءً وأنت بخير تمامًا وتقول *تعال، تعال، تعال* حتى تشعر بالغباء، ولا يحدث شيء. جرّب تيودورو ذلك بالطعام. وجرّبته إينيس أسبوعًا كاملًا في فبراير بدافع الفضول العلميّ المحض فلم تحصل إلا على زكام. ثم في ليلة تكون فيها خائفًا، يكون هناك. وجدّة برونو، وكانت البالغة الوحيدة التي أخبرها، أصغت حتى النهاية ويداها في حوض الغسيل، ثم قالت شيئًا لم يفهمه إلا بعد أربع سنوات تقريبًا. قالت: «ليس القطّ هو الذي يتغيّر.» سألها ماذا يُفترض أن يعني ذلك. جفّفت كوبًا ووضعته على الرفّ ولم تجب مدة طويلة كفت لأن يفترض أنها لن تجيب. «حين كنت فتاة» قالت «كانت في هذا الشارع امرأة لا تستطيع الخروج بعد حلول الظلام البتّة. لا أنها لا تريد — بل لا تستطيع. كان قد حدث لها شيء، من قبل، في مكان آخر، ولم يعرف أحد في الشارع ما هو قطّ، ولم يكن ذلك شأننا. ونحو عشرين سنة، كلّما اضطرّت إلى الذهاب إلى مكان ما مساءً، كان يصادف أن يكون أحد الرجال واقفًا في أعلى الدرج يدخّن سيجارة. رجال مختلفون. لم يتكرّر الرجل نفسه مرتين متتاليتين قطّ. ولم يكن ذلك مدبَّرًا، على حدّ ما استطعت أن أعرف.» «ليس هذا الشيء نفسه.» «لا» وافقت جدّته. «ليس هو.» وأعطته الفوطة ليجفّف بها، وهكذا كانت تنتهي المحادثات في ذلك المطبخ."
          }
        },
        "de": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Er erwähnte es in der Schule und entdeckte im Lauf etwa einer Woche, dass dies kein neues Phänomen war und dass jeder eine etwas andere Fassung davon hatte. Inês hatte eine auf der Treppe hinter dem Markt. Ihre war schwarz, und sie nannte sie Senhor, und sie hatte sie im Januar jeden Abend die Treppe hinaufbegleitet, wenn ihr Vater lange arbeitete. Teodoro sagte, seine sei rot und lebe beim Fußballplatz und tauche nur auf, wenn es regne, wozu er eine Theorie hatte. Die Zwillinge aus dem Jahrgang darüber sagten, sie hätten nie eine Katze gesehen, aber ihre Mutter, als sie klein war, auf der Straße hinaus zum Steinbruch, und dass sie in ihrer Fassung unter einem Auto hervorgekommen und den ganzen Weg bis zur Kreuzung mitgegangen und nicht mehr da gewesen sei, als sie sich umdrehte. Die einzige Regel, die in jeder Fassung vorkam, und Bruno prüfte das nach, war diese: Sie kam nie, wenn man sie rief. Bis Ende Januar hatte er zwölf Berichte gesammelt und schrieb sie hinten in sein Portugiesischheft, unter eine Überschrift, die er später durchstrich, weil sie kindisch aussah. Grau, schwarz, rot, getigert, und eine, bei der Rita nicht nachgab: weiß, mit grauem Gesicht. Verschiedene Straßen. Verschiedene Jahre. Die der Mutter der Zwillinge war von ungefähr 1994, was sie entweder zu einer sehr alten Katze machte oder, wie Teodoro recht laut im Gang bemerkte, nicht zu einer einzigen Katze. Niemand hatte je eine angefasst. Das prüfte er eigens nach, weil er in der ersten Nacht die Hand ausgestreckt hatte und sich danach eine Woche lang dumm dabei vorgekommen war. Niemand hatte. Sie kamen bis auf ungefähr zwei Meter und blieben auf zwei Metern und waren fort, sobald man im Licht war. Auch hatte nie jemand eine ankommen sehen. Das zu bemerken dauerte länger, weil das Fehlen einer solchen Sache schwer zu bemerken ist. In allen zwölf Berichten war die Katze einfach schon da, saß am anderen Ende und sah einen an. Sie kam nicht herangelaufen. Sie kam nicht unter etwas hervor. Sie saß da, an der Stelle, wo das Dunkel aufhörte, und sah einen schon an, bevor man sie ansah. Man konnte um acht Uhr abends, wenn es einem vollkommen gut ging, oben an der Straße stehen und *miez, miez, miez* sagen, bis man sich dumm vorkam, und es geschah nichts. Teodoro hatte es mit Futter versucht. Inês hatte es im Februar aus reiner wissenschaftlicher Neugier eine Woche lang jeden Tag versucht und nichts bekommen außer einer Erkältung. Und dann, an einem Abend, an dem man Angst hatte, war sie da. Brunos Großmutter, die einzige Erwachsene, der er es erzählte, hörte mit den Händen im Spülbecken bis zum Ende zu und sagte dann etwas, das er ungefähr vier Jahre lang nicht verstand. Sie sagte: „Nicht die Katze ist es, die sich ändert.“ Er fragte sie, was das heißen solle. Sie trocknete ein Glas ab und stellte es auf das Gestell und antwortete lange genug nicht, dass er annahm, sie werde es nicht tun. „Als ich ein Mädchen war“, sagte sie, „gab es in dieser Straße eine Frau, die nach Einbruch der Dunkelheit überhaupt nicht hinausgehen konnte. Nicht wollte — konnte. Ihr war früher etwas zugestoßen, anderswo, und niemand in der Straße wusste je, was, und es ging uns nichts an. Und ungefähr zwanzig Jahre lang stand, wann immer sie abends irgendwohin musste, zufällig einer der Männer oben an der Treppe und rauchte eine Zigarette. Verschiedene Männer. Nie zweimal hintereinander derselbe. Nie verabredet, soweit ich das je sagen konnte.“ „Das ist nicht dasselbe.“ „Nein“, stimmte seine Großmutter zu. „Ist es nicht.“ Und sie gab ihm das Tuch zum Abtrocknen, was die Art war, wie Gespräche in dieser Küche endeten."
          }
        },
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "He mentioned it at school and discovered, over about a week, that this was not a new phenomenon and that everybody had a slightly different version of it. Inês had one on the steps behind the market. Hers was black and she called it Senhor and it had walked her up the steps every night in January when her father was working late. Teodoro said his was ginger and lived by the football ground and only turned up when it was raining, which he had a theory about. The twins from the year above said they had never seen a cat but that their mother had, when she was small, on the road out to the quarry, and that in her version it had come out from under a car and gone with her the whole way to the junction and had not been there when she turned round. The only rule that was in every version, and Bruno checked, was this: it never came when you called it. He collected twelve accounts by the end of January and wrote them in the back of his Portuguese copybook, under a heading he later scribbled out because it looked childish. Grey, black, ginger, tabby, and one that Rita would not be moved on: white, with a grey face. Different streets. Different years. The twins' mother's was from about 1994, which made it either a very old cat or, as Teodoro observed at some volume in the corridor, not one cat. Nobody had ever touched one. He checked that specifically, because he had put his hand out on the first night and had felt stupid about it for a week afterwards. Nobody had. They came to about two metres and stayed at two metres and were gone once you were in the light. Nobody had ever seen one arrive, either. That took him longer to notice, because it is a difficult thing to notice the absence of. In all twelve accounts the cat was simply already there, sitting, at the far end, facing you. Not walking up. Not coming out from under something. Sitting, at the place where the dark stopped, already looking at you before you looked at it. You could stand at the top of the street at eight in the evening when you were perfectly fine and say *here, here, here* until you felt stupid, and nothing would happen. Teodoro had tried it with food. Inês had tried it for a week straight in February out of pure scientific curiosity and got nothing but a cold. And then on a night when you were frightened, it would be there. Bruno's grandmother, who was the only adult he told, listened all the way through with her hands in the sink and then said something he did not understand for about four years. She said: \"It isn't the cat that changes.\" He asked her what that was supposed to mean. She dried a glass and put it on the rack and did not answer for long enough that he assumed she was not going to. \"When I was a girl,\" she said, \"there was a woman on this street who could not go out after dark at all. Not would not — could not. Something had happened to her, before, somewhere else, and nobody in the street ever knew what, and it was not our business. And for about twenty years, whenever she had to go anywhere in the evening, one of the men would happen to be standing at the top of the steps having a cigarette. Different men. Never the same one twice in a row. Never arranged, as far as I could ever tell.\" \"That's not the same thing.\" \"No,\" his grandmother agreed. \"It's not.\" And she gave him the cloth to dry with, which was how conversations ended in that kitchen."
          }
        },
        "es": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Lo comentó en el colegio y descubrió, a lo largo de una semana, que aquello no era un fenómeno nuevo y que cada uno tenía una versión ligeramente distinta. Inês tenía uno en las escaleras de detrás del mercado. El suyo era negro y lo llamaba Señor y la había subido las escaleras todas las noches de enero, cuando su padre trabajaba hasta tarde. Teodoro dijo que el suyo era naranja y vivía junto al campo de fútbol y solo aparecía cuando llovía, sobre lo cual tenía una teoría. Las gemelas del curso de arriba dijeron que ellas no habían visto ningún gato pero que su madre sí, de pequeña, en la carretera que va a la cantera, y que en su versión había salido de debajo de un coche y la había acompañado todo el camino hasta el cruce y ya no estaba cuando ella se giró. La única regla que estaba en todas las versiones, y Bruno lo comprobó, era esta: nunca venía cuando lo llamabas. Reunió doce relatos para finales de enero y los escribió al final del cuaderno de portugués, bajo un título que luego tachó porque parecía infantil. Gris, negro, naranja, atigrado, y uno sobre el que Rita no daba su brazo a torcer: blanco, con la cara gris. Calles distintas. Años distintos. El de la madre de las gemelas era de 1994 más o menos, lo que lo hacía o un gato muy viejo o, como observó Teodoro a considerable volumen en el pasillo, no un gato. Nadie había tocado ninguno. Lo comprobó específicamente, porque él había alargado la mano la primera noche y se había sentido tonto durante una semana después. Nadie lo había hecho. Llegaban a unos dos metros y se quedaban a dos metros y desaparecían en cuanto estabas en la luz. Nadie había visto llegar a ninguno, tampoco. Eso tardó más en notarlo, porque una ausencia es difícil de notar. En los doce relatos el gato simplemente ya estaba allí, sentado, al fondo, mirándote. No viniendo andando. No saliendo de debajo de nada. Sentado, en el sitio donde acababa la oscuridad, ya mirándote antes de que tú lo miraras. Podías quedarte en lo alto de la calle a las ocho de la tarde cuando estabas perfectamente bien y decir *ven, ven, ven* hasta sentirte ridículo, y no pasaba nada. Teodoro lo había intentado con comida. Inês lo había intentado una semana seguida en febrero por pura curiosidad científica y no consiguió más que un resfriado. Y entonces una noche en que estabas asustado, estaba allí. La abuela de Bruno, que fue la única adulta a la que se lo contó, escuchó hasta el final con las manos en el fregadero y luego dijo algo que él no entendió hasta unos cuatro años después. Dijo: —No es el gato el que cambia. Él le preguntó qué se suponía que significaba eso. Ella secó un vaso y lo puso en el escurridor y no contestó durante el tiempo suficiente como para que él supusiera que no iba a hacerlo. —Cuando yo era niña —dijo—, había una mujer en esta calle que no podía salir de noche en absoluto. No que no quisiera: no podía. Algo le había pasado, antes, en otro sitio, y nadie en la calle lo supo nunca, y no era asunto nuestro. Y durante unos veinte años, siempre que tenía que ir a algún sitio de noche, uno de los hombres daba la casualidad de que estaba en lo alto de las escaleras fumando un cigarro. Hombres distintos. Nunca el mismo dos veces seguidas. Nunca acordado, hasta donde yo pude ver. —No es lo mismo. —No —convino su abuela—. No lo es. Y le dio el paño para secar, que era como acababan las conversaciones en aquella cocina."
          }
        },
        "fr": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Il en parla à l'école et découvrit, en une semaine environ, que ce n'était pas un phénomène nouveau et que chacun en avait une version légèrement différente. Inês en avait un sur les marches derrière le marché. Le sien était noir et elle l'appelait Senhor et il l'avait accompagnée en haut des marches tous les soirs de janvier pendant que son père travaillait tard. Teodoro dit que le sien était roux et vivait près du stade et ne se montrait que quand il pleuvait, ce sur quoi il avait une théorie. Les jumeaux de la classe au-dessus dirent qu'ils n'avaient jamais vu de chat mais que leur mère en avait vu un, petite, sur la route de la carrière, et que dans sa version il était sorti de sous une voiture et l'avait accompagnée jusqu'au carrefour et n'était plus là quand elle s'était retournée. La seule règle qui figurait dans toutes les versions, et Bruno vérifia, était celle-ci : il ne venait jamais quand on l'appelait. Il rassembla douze récits avant la fin janvier et les écrivit au fond de son cahier de portugais, sous un titre qu'il ratura plus tard parce qu'il faisait enfantin. Gris, noir, roux, tigré, et un sur lequel Rita ne voulut pas céder : blanc, avec la face grise. Des rues différentes. Des années différentes. Celui de la mère des jumeaux datait d'environ 1994, ce qui en faisait soit un chat très vieux, soit, comme Teodoro le fit remarquer à un certain volume dans le couloir, pas un seul chat. Personne n'en avait jamais touché un. Il vérifia ce point précisément, parce qu'il avait tendu la main le premier soir et s'était senti bête pendant une semaine. Personne. Ils venaient à deux mètres environ et restaient à deux mètres et disparaissaient une fois qu'on était dans la lumière. Personne n'en avait jamais vu un arriver, non plus. Cela lui prit plus longtemps à remarquer, parce qu'il est difficile de remarquer l'absence d'une chose. Dans les douze récits, le chat était simplement déjà là, assis, tout au bout, tourné vers vous. Pas en train d'approcher. Pas en train de sortir de sous quelque chose. Assis, à l'endroit où le noir s'arrêtait, déjà en train de vous regarder avant que vous le regardiez. On pouvait se tenir en haut de la rue à huit heures du soir quand on allait parfaitement bien et dire *viens, viens, viens* jusqu'à se sentir ridicule, et rien ne se passait. Teodoro avait essayé avec de la nourriture. Inês avait essayé une semaine d'affilée en février par pure curiosité scientifique et n'avait attrapé qu'un rhume. Et puis, un soir où l'on avait peur, il était là. La grand-mère de Bruno, qui fut le seul adulte à qui il en parla, écouta jusqu'au bout les mains dans l'évier, puis dit une chose qu'il ne comprit pas pendant environ quatre ans. Elle dit : — Ce n'est pas le chat qui change. Il lui demanda ce que cela était censé vouloir dire. Elle essuya un verre et le posa sur l'égouttoir et ne répondit pas assez longtemps pour qu'il en conclue qu'elle n'allait pas répondre. — Quand j'étais petite, dit-elle, il y avait une femme dans cette rue qui ne pouvait pas sortir après la nuit tombée. Pas qui ne voulait pas : qui ne pouvait pas. Quelque chose lui était arrivé, avant, ailleurs, et personne dans la rue n'a jamais su quoi, et cela ne nous regardait pas. Et pendant une vingtaine d'années, chaque fois qu'elle devait aller quelque part le soir, il se trouvait qu'un des hommes était debout en haut des marches à fumer une cigarette. Des hommes différents. Jamais le même deux fois de suite. Jamais arrangé, autant que j'aie jamais pu en juger. — Ce n'est pas la même chose. — Non, convint sa grand-mère. Ce n'est pas la même chose. Et elle lui donna le torchon pour essuyer, ce qui était la façon dont les conversations finissaient dans cette cuisine."
          }
        },
        "it": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Ne accennò a scuola e scoprì, nel giro di una settimana, che non era un fenomeno nuovo e che ognuno ne aveva una versione leggermente diversa. Inês ne aveva uno sulle scalinate dietro il mercato. Il suo era nero e lo chiamava Senhor e l'aveva accompagnata su per le scale tutte le sere di gennaio quando suo padre faceva tardi al lavoro. Teodoro disse che il suo era rosso e viveva vicino al campo da calcio e si faceva vedere solo quando pioveva, cosa su cui aveva una teoria. I gemelli dell'anno sopra dissero che non avevano mai visto un gatto ma che la loro madre sì, da piccola, sulla strada che porta alla cava, e che nella sua versione era sbucato da sotto una macchina ed era andato con lei fino all'incrocio e non c'era più quando si era voltata. L'unica regola presente in tutte le versioni, e Bruno controllò, era questa: non veniva mai quando lo chiamavi. Raccolse dodici racconti entro la fine di gennaio e li scrisse in fondo al quaderno di portoghese, sotto un titolo che poi cancellò con uno scarabocchio perché sembrava infantile. Grigio, nero, rosso, soriano, e uno su cui Rita non si lasciò smuovere: bianco, con il muso grigio. Strade diverse. Anni diversi. Quello della madre dei gemelli era del 1994 circa, il che ne faceva o un gatto molto vecchio o, come osservò Teodoro a un certo volume in corridoio, non un gatto solo. Nessuno ne aveva mai toccato uno. Controllò proprio quello, perché aveva allungato la mano la prima sera e per una settimana dopo si era sentito stupido. Nessuno l'aveva fatto. Arrivavano a circa due metri e restavano a due metri e sparivano una volta che eri nella luce. E nessuno ne aveva mai visto uno arrivare. Se ne accorse più tardi, perché è una cosa di cui è difficile notare l'assenza. In tutti e dodici i racconti il gatto era semplicemente già lì, seduto, in fondo, rivolto verso di te. Non in arrivo. Non che sbucava da sotto qualcosa. Seduto, nel punto in cui il buio finiva, già a guardarti prima che tu guardassi lui. Potevi startene in cima alla strada alle otto di sera quando stavi perfettamente bene e dire *micio, micio, micio* finché non ti sentivi stupido, e non succedeva niente. Teodoro ci aveva provato con il cibo. Inês ci aveva provato per una settimana di fila a febbraio per pura curiosità scientifica e non ne aveva ricavato altro che un raffreddore. E poi, una sera in cui avevi paura, era lì. La nonna di Bruno, che fu l'unica adulta a cui lo raccontò, ascoltò fino in fondo con le mani nel lavello e poi disse una cosa che lui non capì per circa quattro anni. Disse: — Non è il gatto che cambia. Le chiese che cosa dovesse significare. Lei asciugò un bicchiere e lo mise sullo scolapiatti e non rispose per un tempo abbastanza lungo da fargli supporre che non avrebbe risposto. — Quando ero ragazza — disse — c'era una donna in questa strada che dopo il buio non poteva proprio uscire. Non che non volesse: non poteva. Le era successo qualcosa, prima, da un'altra parte, e nessuno nella strada seppe mai cosa, e non erano affari nostri. E per una ventina d'anni, ogni volta che doveva andare da qualche parte la sera, capitava che uno degli uomini fosse in piedi in cima alle scale a fumare una sigaretta. Uomini diversi. Mai lo stesso due volte di fila. Mai concordato, per quanto io sia mai riuscita a capire. — Non è la stessa cosa. — No — ammise sua nonna. — Non lo è. E gli diede lo strofinaccio per asciugare, che era il modo in cui finivano le conversazioni in quella cucina."
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Ele comentou na escola e descobriu, ao longo de mais ou menos uma semana, que aquilo não era um fenômeno novo e que cada um tinha uma versão ligeiramente diferente. Inês tinha um na escadaria atrás do mercado. O dela era preto e ela chamava de Senhor e tinha subido a escadaria com ela toda noite em janeiro, quando o pai estava trabalhando até tarde. Teodoro disse que o dele era laranja e morava perto do campo de futebol e só aparecia quando chovia, sobre o que ele tinha uma teoria. As gêmeas do ano acima disseram que nunca tinham visto gato nenhum mas que a mãe delas tinha visto, quando era pequena, na estrada que vai para a pedreira, e que na versão dela ele tinha saído de debaixo de um carro e ido com ela o caminho todo até o entroncamento e não estava mais lá quando ela se virou. A única regra que estava em todas as versões, e Bruno conferiu, era esta: ele nunca vinha quando você chamava. Ele juntou doze relatos até o fim de janeiro e escreveu no fim do caderno de português, sob um título que depois rabiscou porque parecia infantil. Cinza, preto, laranja, rajado, e um sobre o qual Rita não abria mão: branco, com o rosto cinza. Ruas diferentes. Anos diferentes. O da mãe das gêmeas era de mais ou menos 1994, o que fazia dele ou um gato muito velho ou, como Teodoro observou em considerável volume no corredor, não um gato. Ninguém tinha tocado num. Ele conferiu isso especificamente, porque tinha estendido a mão na primeira noite e tinha se sentido bobo por uma semana depois. Ninguém tinha. Eles chegavam a uns dois metros e ficavam a dois metros e sumiam assim que você estava na luz. Ninguém tinha visto um chegar, tampouco. Isso ele demorou mais a notar, porque é uma coisa difícil de notar, a ausência. Em todos os doze relatos o gato simplesmente já estava lá, sentado, na outra ponta, virado para você. Não vindo andando. Não saindo de debaixo de nada. Sentado, no lugar onde o escuro terminava, já olhando para você antes de você olhar para ele. Você podia ficar no alto da rua às oito da noite quando estava perfeitamente bem e dizer *psiu, psiu, psiu* até se sentir bobo, e não acontecia nada. Teodoro tinha tentado com comida. Inês tinha tentado uma semana seguida em fevereiro por pura curiosidade científica e não ganhou nada além de um resfriado. E então numa noite em que você estava com medo, ele estava lá. A avó de Bruno, que foi a única adulta a quem ele contou, ouviu tudo até o fim com as mãos na pia e depois disse uma coisa que ele não entendeu por uns quatro anos. Disse: — Não é o gato que muda. Ele perguntou o que aquilo deveria significar. Ela secou um copo e pôs no escorredor e não respondeu por tempo suficiente para ele supor que não ia responder. — Quando eu era menina — disse —, havia uma mulher nesta rua que não conseguia sair depois de escurecer de jeito nenhum. Não que não quisesse: não conseguia. Alguma coisa tinha acontecido com ela, antes, em outro lugar, e ninguém na rua nunca soube o quê, e não era da nossa conta. E por uns vinte anos, sempre que ela precisava ir a algum lugar de noite, um dos homens calhava de estar no alto da escadaria fumando um cigarro. Homens diferentes. Nunca o mesmo duas vezes seguidas. Nunca combinado, até onde eu pude perceber. — Não é a mesma coisa. — Não — concordou a avó. — Não é. E deu o pano a ele para secar, que era como as conversas terminavam naquela cozinha."
          }
        }
      }
    },
