    "She held her place over the same field for four days before anyone thought to go and look.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "ar": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "بقيت في مكانها فوق الحقل ذاته أربعة أيام قبل أن يخطر لأحد الذهاب لينظر."
          }
        },
        "de": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Sie hielt vier Tage lang die Stellung über demselben Feld, bevor jemand daran dachte, nachzusehen."
          }
        },
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "She held her place over the same field for four days before anyone thought to go and look."
          }
        },
        "es": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Se mantuvo sobre el mismo campo durante cuatro días antes de que a alguien se le ocurriera ir a mirar."
          }
        },
        "fr": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Elle est restée au même endroit au-dessus du même champ pendant quatre jours avant que quelqu’un ne pense à aller voir."
          }
        },
        "it": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Rimase ferma sopra lo stesso campo per quattro giorni prima che qualcuno pensasse di andare a vedere."
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Ela ficou parada sobre o mesmo campo por quatro dias antes que alguém pensasse em ir olhar."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "She leaves things on the harbour wall. Nobody has ever claimed the small blue shoe.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "ar": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "تترك أشياء على جدار الميناء. لم يطالب أحد بالحذاء الأزرق الصغير."
          }
        },
        "de": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Sie legt Dinge auf der Hafenmauer ab. Niemand hat je den kleinen blauen Schuh abgeholt."
          }
        },
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "She leaves things on the harbour wall. Nobody has ever claimed the small blue shoe."
          }
        },
        "es": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Deja cosas en el muro del puerto. Nadie ha reclamado nunca el zapatito azul."
          }
        },
        "fr": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Elle laisse des choses sur le mur du port. Personne n’a jamais réclamé la petite chaussure bleue."
          }
        },
        "it": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Lascia cose sul muretto del porto. Nessuno ha mai reclamato la scarpetta blu."
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Ela deixa coisas no muro do porto. Ninguém nunca reclamou o sapatinho azul."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "She should not have asked about the four hundred and six. She knew it at the time. The hare had said, very clearly, that it did not enjoy discussing them, and Marisol had heard that and had waited nearly a week, and then had asked anyway, because she was twelve and because a locked drawer is a locked drawer. The hare did not refuse. It went to the corner of the office where the oldest papers were — the ones gone soft and furred at the edges, held down with a flint instead of an acorn — and it pulled one out with its teeth and unrolled it across the slate with a paw at each corner. \"Nineteen forty-six,\" it said. The map showed a stretch of country Marisol half recognised: the valley on the other side of the ridge, before the reservoir, when the reservoir was still a river and three farms. And there was a path on it, drawn firmly, dashed, with a scale bar. It went from a farm gate up over the shoulder of the hill to a chapel. \"That's a good path,\" said the hare. \"That is one of the best I ever did. The gradient is right, it avoids the wet ground in February, it comes out at the chapel from the south so you get the light on the door. A person walking that path would have enjoyed it every single time.\" \"What happened?\" \"Nothing happened. That is precisely the difficulty.\" It sat back. \"The first person did not go. I had them — a woman at the farm, thirty-one, walked to the chapel on a Sunday and back, every week, and the road went round the long way and she resented it. Everything was in place. She had the reason and the temper and the legs.\" The hare's ears went flat and then came up again. \"And in the March her sister got ill in Tarragona, and she went down to nurse her, and she did not come back. That is all. There is no more to it than that.\" Marisol looked at the dashed line going up over the shoulder of the hill to the chapel with the light on the door. \"Couldn't somebody else have gone?\" \"Somebody else did not have her reason. That is what people never understand about this.\" It turned the map round to look at it the right way up, which Marisol thought it probably did not need to do. \"A path is not a good idea. A path is one person's bad afternoon, repeated by strangers who never knew about the afternoon. If you take away the afternoon, there is nothing left to repeat.\" \"So it's just — gone.\" \"It is under a dozen metres of water now, along with the farm and the chapel. But it was gone long before the reservoir. It was gone in the March of nineteen forty-six.\" It rolled the map up. \"I keep them because somebody should. That is the entire reason and I have stopped looking for a better one.\" Marisol sat back on her heels in the wet bracken. \"Doesn't that make you want to stop?\" The hare put the flint back on the roll. \"Four hundred and six,\" it said. \"In two hundred and twelve years. Which means the other ones are out there being walked on, right now, this minute, by people who think the ground has always been like that.\" It went back to the desk. \"You are standing in the light again.\" She moved. She thought about that conversation for a long time afterwards, and the part she came back to was not the woman who went to Tarragona. It was the arithmetic. Two hundred and twelve years, and it had counted the failures precisely and had not, as far as she could tell, counted the others at all.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "She should not have asked about the four hundred and six. She knew it at the time. The hare had said, very clearly, that it did not enjoy discussing them, and Marisol had heard that and had waited nearly a week, and then had asked anyway, because she was twelve and because a locked drawer is a locked drawer. The hare did not refuse. It went to the corner of the office where the oldest papers were — the ones gone soft and furred at the edges, held down with a flint instead of an acorn — and it pulled one out with its teeth and unrolled it across the slate with a paw at each corner. \"Nineteen forty-six,\" it said. The map showed a stretch of country Marisol half recognised: the valley on the other side of the ridge, before the reservoir, when the reservoir was still a river and three farms. And there was a path on it, drawn firmly, dashed, with a scale bar. It went from a farm gate up over the shoulder of the hill to a chapel. \"That's a good path,\" said the hare. \"That is one of the best I ever did. The gradient is right, it avoids the wet ground in February, it comes out at the chapel from the south so you get the light on the door. A person walking that path would have enjoyed it every single time.\" \"What happened?\" \"Nothing happened. That is precisely the difficulty.\" It sat back. \"The first person did not go. I had them — a woman at the farm, thirty-one, walked to the chapel on a Sunday and back, every week, and the road went round the long way and she resented it. Everything was in place. She had the reason and the temper and the legs.\" The hare's ears went flat and then came up again. \"And in the March her sister got ill in Tarragona, and she went down to nurse her, and she did not come back. That is all. There is no more to it than that.\" Marisol looked at the dashed line going up over the shoulder of the hill to the chapel with the light on the door. \"Couldn't somebody else have gone?\" \"Somebody else did not have her reason. That is what people never understand about this.\" It turned the map round to look at it the right way up, which Marisol thought it probably did not need to do. \"A path is not a good idea. A path is one person's bad afternoon, repeated by strangers who never knew about the afternoon. If you take away the afternoon, there is nothing left to repeat.\" \"So it's just — gone.\" \"It is under a dozen metres of water now, along with the farm and the chapel. But it was gone long before the reservoir. It was gone in the March of nineteen forty-six.\" It rolled the map up. \"I keep them because somebody should. That is the entire reason and I have stopped looking for a better one.\" Marisol sat back on her heels in the wet bracken. \"Doesn't that make you want to stop?\" The hare put the flint back on the roll. \"Four hundred and six,\" it said. \"In two hundred and twelve years. Which means the other ones are out there being walked on, right now, this minute, by people who think the ground has always been like that.\" It went back to the desk. \"You are standing in the light again.\" She moved. She thought about that conversation for a long time afterwards, and the part she came back to was not the woman who went to Tarragona. It was the arithmetic. Two hundred and twelve years, and it had counted the failures precisely and had not, as far as she could tell, counted the others at all."
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Ela não devia ter perguntado sobre os quatrocentos e seis. Sabia disso na hora. A lebre tinha dito, com toda a clareza, que não gostava de discutir o assunto, e Marisol tinha ouvido e tinha esperado quase uma semana, e depois tinha perguntado assim mesmo, porque tinha doze anos e porque uma gaveta trancada é uma gaveta trancada. A lebre não se recusou. Foi até o canto do escritório onde estavam os papéis mais velhos — os que tinham ficado moles e felpudos nas bordas, presos com uma pederneira em vez de uma bolota — e puxou um com os dentes e desenrolou sobre a ardósia com uma pata em cada canto. — Mil novecentos e quarenta e seis — disse. O mapa mostrava um trecho de campo que Marisol meio que reconheceu: o vale do outro lado da crista, antes da represa, quando a represa ainda era um rio e três fazendas. E havia um caminho nele, desenhado com firmeza, tracejado, com uma barra de escala. Ia de um portão de fazenda por cima do ombro do morro até uma capela. — Esse é um bom caminho — disse a lebre. — É um dos melhores que já fiz. A inclinação está certa, evita o terreno encharcado em fevereiro, chega à capela pelo sul, então você pega a luz na porta. Uma pessoa andando por esse caminho teria gostado dele todas as vezes. — O que aconteceu? — Nada aconteceu. É exatamente essa a dificuldade. Recostou-se. — A primeira pessoa não foi. Eu a tinha — uma mulher da fazenda, trinta e um anos, ia a pé até a capela no domingo e voltava, toda semana, e a estrada dava a volta comprida e ela se ressentia disso. Estava tudo no lugar. Ela tinha o motivo e o mau humor e as pernas. As orelhas da lebre se abaixaram e depois se levantaram de novo. — E em março a irmã dela adoeceu em Tarragona, e ela desceu para cuidar dela, e não voltou. É só isso. Não há mais nada além disso. Marisol olhou para a linha tracejada subindo por cima do ombro do morro até a capela com a luz na porta. — Outra pessoa não podia ter ido? — Outra pessoa não tinha o motivo dela. É isso que as pessoas nunca entendem a respeito disso. Virou o mapa para olhá-lo do lado certo, o que Marisol achou que ela provavelmente não precisava fazer. — Um caminho não é uma boa ideia. Um caminho é a tarde ruim de uma pessoa, repetida por estranhos que nunca souberam da tarde. Se você tira a tarde, não sobra nada para repetir. — Então ele simplesmente — sumiu. — Está debaixo de uns doze metros de água agora, junto com a fazenda e a capela. Mas tinha sumido muito antes da represa. Sumiu em março de mil novecentos e quarenta e seis. Enrolou o mapa. — Eu os guardo porque alguém deveria. É essa a razão inteira e parei de procurar uma melhor. Marisol sentou sobre os calcanhares na samambaia molhada. — Isso não dá vontade de parar? A lebre pôs a pederneira de volta sobre o rolo. — Quatrocentos e seis — disse. — Em duzentos e doze anos. O que quer dizer que os outros estão por aí sendo pisados, agora, neste minuto, por gente que acha que o chão sempre foi assim. Voltou para a escrivaninha. — Você está na minha luz de novo. Ela saiu do caminho. Pensou naquela conversa por muito tempo depois, e a parte a que voltava não era a mulher que foi para Tarragona. Era a aritmética. Duzentos e doze anos, e ela tinha contado os fracassos com precisão e não tinha, até onde Marisol podia ver, contado os outros de jeito nenhum."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "She walked it that afternoon. Stile to ridge, the way the map had it, and it was awful. The bracken came up past her shoulders in the middle stretch and it did not want her there. It closed behind her. It got down her collar. There was a bank two thirds of the way up that was not marked on the map at all and that she had to go up on her hands, and there was a wet patch in a dip near the middle where her boot went in to the ankle and came out with a noise like a kiss. It took her an hour and ten minutes. The bottom path takes twenty. She did it again on the Thursday, because she had left her jumper at the top. That was all. That was the entire reason. She was cross about the jumper and she went back up the way she knew rather than the way that was sensible, and the second time it took fifty minutes because the bracken in the middle stretch was already bent. In August her cousins came from the coast, three of them, and she took them up that way because she said it was the fastest route to the view. That was a lie. She knew it was a lie when she said it. She has never entirely worked out why she said it — some part of her wanted them to go that way, and the reason was small and private, exactly as the hare had said it would be. By the following spring there was a line of flattened green from the stile to the ridge, visible from the road if you knew to look. By the spring after that there was bare earth showing in it in three places. The year after that somebody put a plank across the wet part near the middle. Nobody knows who. Marisol asked four people and they all assumed it had been there for years. She is grown up now, and she went back once, in her thirties, on a cold bright day in February when the bracken was down and everything was visible. The path is a path. It has a sign at the bottom, on the stile, a small green one with the mileage and a little picture of a boot. It says 800m, which she thought was a nice touch, or would have been if anyone else had been there to say it to. She went along the top edge of the wood afterwards, where the bracken thins, and she looked for a long time for a beech with a gap between two roots. She found several beeches. She found several gaps. She was not, in the end, entirely surprised, and she did not stay long, because the light was going and she had a train.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "She walked it that afternoon. Stile to ridge, the way the map had it, and it was awful. The bracken came up past her shoulders in the middle stretch and it did not want her there. It closed behind her. It got down her collar. There was a bank two thirds of the way up that was not marked on the map at all and that she had to go up on her hands, and there was a wet patch in a dip near the middle where her boot went in to the ankle and came out with a noise like a kiss. It took her an hour and ten minutes. The bottom path takes twenty. She did it again on the Thursday, because she had left her jumper at the top. That was all. That was the entire reason. She was cross about the jumper and she went back up the way she knew rather than the way that was sensible, and the second time it took fifty minutes because the bracken in the middle stretch was already bent. In August her cousins came from the coast, three of them, and she took them up that way because she said it was the fastest route to the view. That was a lie. She knew it was a lie when she said it. She has never entirely worked out why she said it — some part of her wanted them to go that way, and the reason was small and private, exactly as the hare had said it would be. By the following spring there was a line of flattened green from the stile to the ridge, visible from the road if you knew to look. By the spring after that there was bare earth showing in it in three places. The year after that somebody put a plank across the wet part near the middle. Nobody knows who. Marisol asked four people and they all assumed it had been there for years. She is grown up now, and she went back once, in her thirties, on a cold bright day in February when the bracken was down and everything was visible. The path is a path. It has a sign at the bottom, on the stile, a small green one with the mileage and a little picture of a boot. It says 800m, which she thought was a nice touch, or would have been if anyone else had been there to say it to. She went along the top edge of the wood afterwards, where the bracken thins, and she looked for a long time for a beech with a gap between two roots. She found several beeches. She found several gaps. She was not, in the end, entirely surprised, and she did not stay long, because the light was going and she had a train."
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Ela fez o percurso naquela tarde. Da escadinha até a crista, do jeito que o mapa dizia, e foi horrível. A samambaia passava dos ombros dela no trecho do meio e não a queria ali. Fechava atrás dela. Entrava pela gola. Havia um barranco a dois terços da subida que não estava marcado no mapa de jeito nenhum e que ela teve que subir com as mãos, e havia um lugar encharcado numa depressão perto do meio onde a bota dela afundou até o tornozelo e saiu com um barulho de beijo. Levou uma hora e dez minutos. O caminho de baixo leva vinte. Ela fez de novo na quinta-feira, porque tinha esquecido o suéter lá em cima. Foi só isso. Foi essa a razão inteira. Estava irritada por causa do suéter e voltou pelo caminho que conhecia em vez do que era sensato, e na segunda vez levou cinquenta minutos porque a samambaia do trecho do meio já estava dobrada. Em agosto os primos dela vieram do litoral, três deles, e ela os levou por ali porque disse que era a rota mais rápida até a vista. Era mentira. Ela sabia que era mentira quando disse. Nunca descobriu inteiramente por que disse — alguma parte dela queria que eles fossem por ali, e o motivo era pequeno e particular, exatamente como a lebre tinha dito que seria. Na primavera seguinte havia uma linha de verde amassado da escadinha até a crista, visível da estrada se você soubesse onde olhar. Na primavera depois daquela havia terra à mostra nela em três pontos. No ano seguinte alguém pôs uma tábua sobre a parte encharcada perto do meio. Ninguém sabe quem. Marisol perguntou a quatro pessoas e todas supuseram que a tábua estava lá havia anos. Ela é adulta agora, e voltou uma vez, na casa dos trinta, num dia frio e claro de fevereiro em que a samambaia estava baixa e tudo era visível. O caminho é um caminho. Tem uma placa embaixo, na escadinha, uma placinha verde com a quilometragem e um desenhinho de uma bota. Diz 800m, o que ela achou um toque bonito, ou teria achado se houvesse mais alguém ali para quem dizer isso. Depois foi pela borda de cima do bosque, onde a samambaia rareia, e procurou por muito tempo uma faia com um vão entre duas raízes. Encontrou várias faias. Encontrou vários vãos. No fim das contas, não ficou inteiramente surpresa, e não ficou muito tempo, porque a luz estava indo embora e ela tinha um trem para pegar."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "She went up again the next night, and the night after that. By the fourth night she had worked out how to pick one up. You do not catch a moth; you put your finger against the wall beside it and wait, and eventually it walks onto you, unhurried, like somebody stepping onto a bus. The one that came onto her finger on the fourth night had a hallway painted across its wings. A long hallway with a runner carpet, and at the end of it, a green door. She held it under the bulb and looked at it for a long time. The longer she looked, the more certain she became that she had walked down that hallway. She knew what the light was like at the far end in the afternoon. She knew the carpet had a worn patch two thirds of the way along and that you could feel it through socks. And at exactly the same time, with exactly the same certainty, she knew that she had never been there in her life. Her uncle came up the ladder behind her with two mugs of tea, which is a difficult thing to do and suggested practice. He did not seem surprised by any of it. He handed her a mug, sat down on an upturned crate that had clearly been sat on many times before, and looked at the moth on her finger. \"Green door,\" he said. \"That's a good one. Somebody misses that.\" Ines said nothing, because she could not think of a question small enough to start with. \"People drop dreams,\" her uncle said. \"All the time. The way you drop a glove out of a pocket — you don't feel it go, you just find later that you've only got the one. Wake up too fast, somebody says your name, the alarm goes off in the wrong part of it. And the dream falls out.\" He drank some tea. \"They have to go somewhere. Everything has to go somewhere. This house has been collecting them since before it was mine, and before it was my mother's, and I've never worked out why this house and not the one next door.\" \"How many are there?\" \"I stopped counting in about nineteen ninety.\" Ines turned her hand slowly so the moth stayed level. On the wall above her, a hundred pale wings held a hundred small pictures: a bicycle, a hospital corridor, a hillside with a wall across it, a wedding, a dog, a shop she was almost sure was the shop on Bridge Street but with different windows. \"Can they go back?\" she said. \"Can you give one back to someone?\" Her uncle looked at his tea for a while. \"That,\" he said, \"is the difficult part.\"": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "ar": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "صعدت مرة أخرى في الليلة التالية، وفي الليلة التي بعدها. وبحلول الليلة الرابعة كانت قد اهتدت إلى كيفية التقاط واحدة. أنت لا تمسك فراشة؛ بل تضع إصبعك على الجدار إلى جانبها وتنتظر، فتمشي إليك في النهاية، غير متعجّلة، كمن يصعد إلى حافلة. التي جاءت إلى إصبعها في الليلة الرابعة كان على جناحيها رواق مرسوم. رواق طويل فيه سجّادة ممدودة، وفي آخره باب أخضر. أمسكتها تحت المصباح ونظرت إليها وقتًا طويلًا. وكلّما أطالت النظر ازدادت يقينًا أنها مشت في ذلك الرواق. كانت تعرف كيف يكون الضوء في طرفه البعيد بعد الظهر. وكانت تعرف أنّ في السجّادة موضعًا مهترئًا عند ثلثي طولها، وأنك تحسّه من تحت الجوارب. وفي الوقت نفسه تمامًا، وباليقين نفسه تمامًا، كانت تعرف أنها لم تكن هناك قطّ في حياتها. صعد عمّها السلّم خلفها ومعه كوبا شاي، وهو أمر صعب يدلّ على تمرّس. لم يبدُ عليه أنه مندهش من شيء من ذلك. ناولها كوبًا، وجلس على صندوق مقلوب كان واضحًا أنه جُلس عليه مرات كثيرة من قبل، ونظر إلى الفراشة على إصبعها. «باب أخضر» قال. «هذه جيّدة. أحدهم يفتقد ذلك.» لم تقل إينيس شيئًا، لأنها لم تجد سؤالًا صغيرًا بما يكفي لتبدأ به. «الناس يُسقطون الأحلام» قال عمّها. «طوال الوقت. كما تُسقط قفّازًا من جيبك — لا تشعر به وهو يذهب، وإنما تجد بعد حين أنه لم يبقَ معك إلا واحد. تستيقظ بسرعة أكثر مما ينبغي، أو ينادي أحدهم اسمك، أو يرنّ المنبّه في الجزء الخطأ منه. فيسقط الحلم.» شرب بعض الشاي. «لا بدّ أن تذهب إلى مكان ما. كلّ شيء لا بدّ أن يذهب إلى مكان ما. هذا البيت يجمعها من قبل أن يكون لي، ومن قبل أن يكون لأمّي، ولم أهتدِ قطّ إلى سبب أن يكون هذا البيت لا البيت المجاور.» «كم عددها؟» «كففت عن العدّ نحو سنة ألف وتسعمئة وتسعين.» أدارت إينيس يدها ببطء كي تبقى الفراشة مستوية. وعلى الجدار فوقها، كان مئة جناح شاحب يحمل مئة صورة صغيرة: درّاجة، ورواق مستشفى، وسفح تلّ يقطعه جدار، وعرس، وكلب، ودكّان كانت تكاد تجزم أنه الدكّان الذي في Bridge Street لكن بنوافذ مختلفة. «هل يمكن أن تعود؟» قالت. «هل يمكن أن تُعيد واحدة إلى صاحبها؟» نظر عمّها إلى شايه بُرهة. «تلك» قال «هي المسألة الصعبة.»"
          }
        },
        "de": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Sie stieg in der nächsten Nacht wieder hinauf, und in der Nacht darauf. In der vierten Nacht hatte sie herausgefunden, wie man eine aufnimmt. Man fängt eine Motte nicht; man legt den Finger neben sie an die Wand und wartet, und irgendwann läuft sie auf einen herauf, ohne Eile, wie jemand, der in einen Bus steigt. Die, die ihr in der vierten Nacht auf den Finger kam, hatte einen Flur über die Flügel gemalt. Einen langen Flur mit einem Läufer, und an seinem Ende eine grüne Tür. Sie hielt sie unter die Glühbirne und sah sie lange an. Je länger sie hinsah, desto sicherer wurde sie, dass sie diesen Flur entlanggegangen war. Sie wusste, wie das Licht am anderen Ende am Nachmittag war. Sie wusste, dass der Läufer nach zwei Dritteln eine abgetretene Stelle hatte und dass man sie durch Socken spürte. Und zur genau gleichen Zeit, mit genau der gleichen Sicherheit, wusste sie, dass sie nie in ihrem Leben dort gewesen war. Ihr Onkel kam mit zwei Bechern Tee hinter ihr die Leiter herauf, was schwierig ist und auf Übung schließen ließ. Nichts davon schien ihn zu überraschen. Er reichte ihr einen Becher, setzte sich auf eine umgedrehte Kiste, auf der offensichtlich schon oft gesessen worden war, und sah die Motte auf ihrem Finger an. „Grüne Tür“, sagte er. „Das ist eine gute. Die fehlt jemandem.“ Ines sagte nichts, weil ihr keine Frage einfiel, die klein genug zum Anfangen war. „Die Leute verlieren Träume“, sagte ihr Onkel. „Dauernd. So wie einem ein Handschuh aus der Tasche fällt — man merkt es nicht, man stellt nur später fest, dass man nur noch den einen hat. Man wacht zu schnell auf, jemand sagt deinen Namen, der Wecker klingelt an der falschen Stelle. Und der Traum fällt heraus.“ Er trank etwas Tee. „Sie müssen irgendwohin. Alles muss irgendwohin. Dieses Haus sammelt sie schon, seit es noch nicht meins war, und schon bevor es meiner Mutter gehörte, und ich habe nie herausgefunden, warum dieses Haus und nicht das nebenan.“ „Wie viele sind es?“ „Ich habe irgendwann um neunzehnhundertneunzig aufgehört zu zählen.“ Ines drehte die Hand langsam, damit die Motte waagerecht blieb. An der Wand über ihr trugen hundert blasse Flügel hundert kleine Bilder: ein Fahrrad, ein Krankenhausflur, ein Hang mit einer Mauer quer darüber, eine Hochzeit, ein Hund, ein Laden, von dem sie fast sicher war, dass es der Laden in der Bridge Street war, aber mit anderen Fenstern. „Können sie zurück?“, sagte sie. „Kann man einem Menschen einen zurückgeben?“ Ihr Onkel sah eine Weile in seinen Tee. „Das“, sagte er, „ist der schwierige Teil.“"
          }
        },
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "She went up again the next night, and the night after that. By the fourth night she had worked out how to pick one up. You do not catch a moth; you put your finger against the wall beside it and wait, and eventually it walks onto you, unhurried, like somebody stepping onto a bus. The one that came onto her finger on the fourth night had a hallway painted across its wings. A long hallway with a runner carpet, and at the end of it, a green door. She held it under the bulb and looked at it for a long time. The longer she looked, the more certain she became that she had walked down that hallway. She knew what the light was like at the far end in the afternoon. She knew the carpet had a worn patch two thirds of the way along and that you could feel it through socks. And at exactly the same time, with exactly the same certainty, she knew that she had never been there in her life. Her uncle came up the ladder behind her with two mugs of tea, which is a difficult thing to do and suggested practice. He did not seem surprised by any of it. He handed her a mug, sat down on an upturned crate that had clearly been sat on many times before, and looked at the moth on her finger. \"Green door,\" he said. \"That's a good one. Somebody misses that.\" Ines said nothing, because she could not think of a question small enough to start with. \"People drop dreams,\" her uncle said. \"All the time. The way you drop a glove out of a pocket — you don't feel it go, you just find later that you've only got the one. Wake up too fast, somebody says your name, the alarm goes off in the wrong part of it. And the dream falls out.\" He drank some tea. \"They have to go somewhere. Everything has to go somewhere. This house has been collecting them since before it was mine, and before it was my mother's, and I've never worked out why this house and not the one next door.\" \"How many are there?\" \"I stopped counting in about nineteen ninety.\" Ines turned her hand slowly so the moth stayed level. On the wall above her, a hundred pale wings held a hundred small pictures: a bicycle, a hospital corridor, a hillside with a wall across it, a wedding, a dog, a shop she was almost sure was the shop on Bridge Street but with different windows. \"Can they go back?\" she said. \"Can you give one back to someone?\" Her uncle looked at his tea for a while. \"That,\" he said, \"is the difficult part.\""
          }
        },
        "es": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Subió otra vez la noche siguiente, y la noche después. Para la cuarta noche ya había averiguado cómo coger una. A una polilla no se la atrapa; pones el dedo contra la pared a su lado y esperas, y tarde o temprano se sube a ti, sin prisa, como alguien subiendo a un autobús. La que se le subió al dedo la cuarta noche tenía un pasillo pintado en las alas. Un pasillo largo con una alfombra de tira, y al final, una puerta verde. La sostuvo bajo la bombilla y la miró un buen rato. Cuanto más miraba, más segura estaba de haber recorrido aquel pasillo. Sabía cómo era la luz al fondo por la tarde. Sabía que la alfombra tenía una zona gastada a dos tercios del camino y que se notaba a través de los calcetines. Y exactamente al mismo tiempo, con exactamente la misma certeza, sabía que nunca había estado allí en su vida. Su tío subió la escalera detrás de ella con dos tazas de té, lo cual es difícil de hacer y sugería práctica. No pareció sorprendido por nada de aquello. Le dio una taza, se sentó en una caja del revés en la que claramente se había sentado muchas veces, y miró la polilla en el dedo de Ines. —Puerta verde —dijo—. Esa es buena. Alguien echa esa de menos. Ines no dijo nada, porque no se le ocurría una pregunta lo bastante pequeña para empezar. —La gente se deja caer sueños —dijo su tío—. Todo el tiempo. Como se te cae un guante del bolsillo: no lo notas irse, solo descubres luego que te ha quedado uno. Despertarse demasiado rápido, que alguien diga tu nombre, que suene el despertador en la parte equivocada. Y el sueño se cae. —Bebió un poco de té—. Tienen que ir a alguna parte. Todo tiene que ir a alguna parte. Esta casa lleva recogiéndolos desde antes de ser mía, y antes de ser de mi madre, y nunca he averiguado por qué esta casa y no la de al lado. —¿Cuántas hay? —Dejé de contar hacia mil novecientos noventa. Ines giró la mano despacio para que la polilla quedara nivelada. En la pared, encima de ella, cien alas pálidas sostenían cien cuadros pequeños: una bicicleta, un pasillo de hospital, una ladera con un muro cruzándola, una boda, un perro, una tienda que estaba casi segura de que era la tienda de la calle del Puente pero con otras ventanas. —¿Pueden volver? —dijo—. ¿Se le puede devolver una a alguien? Su tío miró su té un rato. —Eso —dijo— es la parte difícil."
          }
        },
        "fr": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Elle remonta la nuit suivante, et celle d'après. À la quatrième nuit, elle avait compris comment en prendre un. On n'attrape pas un papillon de nuit ; on pose le doigt contre le mur à côté de lui et on attend, et il finit par monter sur vous, sans se presser, comme quelqu'un qui monte dans un bus. Celui qui vint sur son doigt la quatrième nuit avait un couloir peint sur les ailes. Un long couloir avec un tapis de passage, et au bout, une porte verte. Elle le tint sous l'ampoule et le regarda longtemps. Plus elle regardait, plus elle devenait certaine d'avoir marché dans ce couloir. Elle savait quelle lumière il y avait au fond l'après-midi. Elle savait que le tapis avait une usure aux deux tiers et qu'on la sentait à travers les chaussettes. Et exactement en même temps, avec exactement la même certitude, elle savait qu'elle n'y avait jamais mis les pieds de sa vie. Son oncle monta l'échelle derrière elle avec deux tasses de thé, ce qui est difficile à faire et suppose de l'entraînement. Rien de tout cela ne parut le surprendre. Il lui tendit une tasse, s'assit sur une caisse retournée sur laquelle on s'était visiblement assis bien des fois, et regarda le papillon sur son doigt. — Porte verte, dit-il. C'en est un bon. Quelqu'un regrette celui-là. Ines ne dit rien, parce qu'elle ne trouvait pas de question assez petite pour commencer. — Les gens laissent tomber des rêves, dit son oncle. Tout le temps. Comme on laisse tomber un gant d'une poche : on ne le sent pas partir, on s'aperçoit seulement plus tard qu'on n'en a plus qu'un. On se réveille trop vite, quelqu'un dit votre nom, le réveil sonne au mauvais moment du rêve. Et le rêve tombe. Il but un peu de thé. — Il faut bien qu'ils aillent quelque part. Tout doit aller quelque part. Cette maison les collectionne depuis avant qu'elle soit à moi, et avant qu'elle soit à ma mère, et je n'ai jamais compris pourquoi cette maison-ci et pas celle d'à côté. — Il y en a combien ? — J'ai arrêté de compter vers mille neuf cent quatre-vingt-dix. Ines tourna lentement la main pour que le papillon reste à plat. Sur le mur au-dessus d'elle, cent ailes pâles tenaient cent petites images : une bicyclette, un couloir d'hôpital, un flanc de colline avec un mur en travers, un mariage, un chien, une boutique dont elle était presque sûre que c'était celle de la rue du Pont mais avec d'autres vitrines. — Est-ce qu'ils peuvent retourner ? dit-elle. Est-ce qu'on peut en rendre un à quelqu'un ? Son oncle regarda son thé un moment. — Ça, dit-il, c'est la partie difficile."
          }
        },
        "it": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Risalì la notte dopo, e la notte dopo ancora. Alla quarta notte aveva capito come prenderne una in mano. Una falena non si acchiappa; si appoggia il dito al muro accanto a lei e si aspetta, e prima o poi lei ti cammina sopra, senza fretta, come qualcuno che sale su un autobus. Quella che le salì sul dito la quarta notte aveva un corridoio dipinto sulle ali. Un corridoio lungo con una passatoia, e in fondo, una porta verde. La tenne sotto la lampadina e la guardò a lungo. Più guardava, più diventava certa di aver percorso quel corridoio. Sapeva com'era la luce in fondo nel pomeriggio. Sapeva che la passatoia aveva una zona consumata a due terzi del percorso e che la si sentiva attraverso i calzini. E nello stesso identico momento, con la stessa identica certezza, sapeva di non esserci mai stata in vita sua. Suo zio salì la scala dietro di lei con due tazze di tè, che è una cosa difficile da fare e lasciava intendere una certa pratica. Non sembrò sorpreso da niente di tutto ciò. Le porse una tazza, si sedette su una cassetta rovesciata su cui era chiaro che si fosse seduto molte altre volte, e guardò la falena sul suo dito. — Porta verde — disse. — Quella è delle buone. A qualcuno manca. Ines non disse niente, perché non le veniva in mente una domanda abbastanza piccola da cui cominciare. — La gente lascia cadere i sogni — disse suo zio. — Di continuo. Come ti cade un guanto da una tasca: non lo senti andare via, scopri solo più tardi che te n'è rimasto uno solo. Ti svegli troppo in fretta, qualcuno dice il tuo nome, la sveglia suona nel punto sbagliato. E il sogno cade fuori. — Bevve un po' di tè. — Da qualche parte devono andare. Tutto deve andare da qualche parte. Questa casa li raccoglie da prima che fosse mia, e da prima che fosse di mia madre, e non ho mai capito perché questa casa e non quella accanto. — Quante sono? — Ho smesso di contarle intorno al millenovecentonovanta. Ines girò la mano lentamente perché la falena restasse in piano. Sul muro sopra di lei, cento ali pallide reggevano cento piccole immagini: una bicicletta, un corridoio d'ospedale, un fianco di collina con un muro di traverso, un matrimonio, un cane, un negozio che era quasi sicura fosse il negozio di Bridge Street ma con vetrine diverse. — Possono tornare indietro? — chiese. — Si può ridarne una a qualcuno? Suo zio guardò il suo tè per un po'. — Quella — disse — è la parte difficile."
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Ela subiu de novo na noite seguinte, e na noite depois dessa. Na quarta noite já tinha descoberto como pegar uma. Você não pega uma mariposa; você encosta o dedo na parede ao lado dela e espera, e mais cedo ou mais tarde ela caminha para cima de você, sem pressa, como alguém subindo num ônibus. A que subiu no dedo dela na quarta noite tinha um corredor pintado nas asas. Um corredor comprido com uma passadeira, e no fim dele, uma porta verde. Ela segurou sob a lâmpada e olhou por um tempo longo. Quanto mais olhava, mais certeza tinha de que já tinha andado por aquele corredor. Sabia como era a luz na outra ponta à tarde. Sabia que a passadeira tinha uma parte gasta a dois terços do caminho e que dava para sentir através da meia. E exatamente ao mesmo tempo, com exatamente a mesma certeza, sabia que nunca tinha estado ali na vida. O tio subiu a escada atrás dela com duas canecas de chá, o que é uma coisa difícil de fazer e sugeria prática. Ele não pareceu surpreso com nada daquilo. Passou uma caneca para ela, sentou num engradado virado que claramente já tinha servido de assento muitas vezes, e olhou para a mariposa no dedo dela. — Porta verde — disse. — Essa é boa. Alguém sente falta dessa. Ines não disse nada, porque não conseguia pensar numa pergunta pequena o bastante para começar. — As pessoas deixam cair sonhos — disse o tio. — O tempo todo. Do jeito que se deixa cair uma luva do bolso: você não sente ir, só descobre depois que ficou só com uma. Acordar rápido demais, alguém falar seu nome, o despertador tocar na parte errada. E o sonho cai. — Bebeu um pouco de chá. — Eles têm que ir para algum lugar. Tudo tem que ir para algum lugar. Esta casa está recolhendo eles desde antes de ser minha, e antes de ser da minha mãe, e eu nunca entendi por que esta casa e não a do lado. — Quantas são? — Parei de contar lá por mil novecentos e noventa. Ines girou a mão devagar para a mariposa ficar nivelada. Na parede acima dela, cem asas pálidas seguravam cem quadros pequenos: uma bicicleta, um corredor de hospital, uma encosta com um muro atravessado, um casamento, um cachorro, uma loja que ela tinha quase certeza de ser a loja da Rua da Ponte mas com outras janelas. — Elas podem voltar? — disse. — Dá para devolver uma para alguém? O tio olhou para o chá um tempo. — Essa — disse — é a parte difícil."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "She will not put it down until somebody in the valley asks her to, properly.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "ar": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "لن تُلقيَه حتى يطلب منها أحدهم في الوادي، كما ينبغي."
          }
        },
        "de": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Sie legt es nicht ab, bis jemand im Tal sie darum bittet, richtig."
          }
        },
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "She will not put it down until somebody in the valley asks her to, properly."
          }
        },
        "es": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "No lo soltará hasta que alguien en el valle se lo pida, como se debe."
          }
        },
        "fr": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Elle ne le déposera pas tant que quelqu’un dans la vallée ne le lui aura pas demandé, comme il faut."
          }
        },
        "it": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Non lo lascerà finché qualcuno nella valle non glielo chiederà, come si deve."
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Ela não vai soltar até que alguém no vale peça, direito."
