    "Spring is four weeks late, and Tolek is the only one who goes up the mountain to find out why.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "ar": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "الربيع متأخر أربعة أسابيع، وتوليك هو الوحيد الذي يصعد الجبل ليعرف السبب."
          }
        },
        "de": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Der Frühling ist vier Wochen zu spät, und Tolek ist der Einzige, der auf den Berg steigt, um herauszufinden warum."
          }
        },
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Spring is four weeks late, and Tolek is the only one who goes up the mountain to find out why."
          }
        },
        "es": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "La primavera lleva cuatro semanas de retraso, y Tolek es el único que sube a la montaña para averiguar por qué."
          }
        },
        "fr": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Le printemps a quatre semaines de retard, et Tolek est le seul à monter dans la montagne pour comprendre."
          }
        },
        "it": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "La primavera è in ritardo di quattro settimane, e Tolek è l’unico a salire in montagna per scoprire perché."
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "A primavera está atrasada em quatro semanas, e Tolek é o único que sobe a montanha para descobrir por quê."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "Spring was four weeks late and everyone in the valley had a theory. Mr Varga at the garage said it was the jet stream, and said it in the tone of a man who had recently learned what a jet stream was. The women who came up from Brod on the Thursday bus said it was because the winter had been mild and the year was making up for it. Father Emil said it was not for us to know, which Tolek noticed was the answer Father Emil gave to almost everything, and which had the advantage of always being technically correct. Tolek's mother said the weather owed nobody an explanation, and told him to bring the washing in. But the almond tree by the church had put out buds in February and then thought better of it. That was the part nobody had a theory for. The buds had come, small and pink at the tips, in a warm week at the end of winter. And then they had stopped. They had not opened and they had not fallen. They had simply shut, and gone hard, and stayed like that on the branches for nine weeks — hundreds of little closed fists, waiting. Old trees do not do that. Old trees commit. And the snow on the high field was not melting. It was not falling either. That was the other thing. There had been no new snow since the second week of March, and there had been sun, real sun, enough to warm the stones on the south wall. But the white line across the top of the mountain had not moved down by so much as a metre. It sat where it was, exactly where it was, with an edge as clean as a page. Tolek walked up to it after school on a Tuesday, with his hands pulled inside his sleeves. He was not being brave. He wanted to know, and the wanting had got bigger than the not-wanting over about fourteen days, which is how most brave things actually happen. At the edge of the snow he stopped and looked at the ground. There were tracks going up. They were enormous — deep, round, with the claw marks well out in front of the pad — and there were no tracks coming down. Not old ones filled in with new snow, because there had been no new snow. None at all. He stood there for a while doing the arithmetic that everybody does at that moment, which is: *if it went up, and it has not come down, then it is up there.* Then he followed the tracks, because he was twelve, and because some questions will not wait until you are older.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Spring was four weeks late and everyone in the valley had a theory. Mr Varga at the garage said it was the jet stream, and said it in the tone of a man who had recently learned what a jet stream was. The women who came up from Brod on the Thursday bus said it was because the winter had been mild and the year was making up for it. Father Emil said it was not for us to know, which Tolek noticed was the answer Father Emil gave to almost everything, and which had the advantage of always being technically correct. Tolek's mother said the weather owed nobody an explanation, and told him to bring the washing in. But the almond tree by the church had put out buds in February and then thought better of it. That was the part nobody had a theory for. The buds had come, small and pink at the tips, in a warm week at the end of winter. And then they had stopped. They had not opened and they had not fallen. They had simply shut, and gone hard, and stayed like that on the branches for nine weeks — hundreds of little closed fists, waiting. Old trees do not do that. Old trees commit. And the snow on the high field was not melting. It was not falling either. That was the other thing. There had been no new snow since the second week of March, and there had been sun, real sun, enough to warm the stones on the south wall. But the white line across the top of the mountain had not moved down by so much as a metre. It sat where it was, exactly where it was, with an edge as clean as a page. Tolek walked up to it after school on a Tuesday, with his hands pulled inside his sleeves. He was not being brave. He wanted to know, and the wanting had got bigger than the not-wanting over about fourteen days, which is how most brave things actually happen. At the edge of the snow he stopped and looked at the ground. There were tracks going up. They were enormous — deep, round, with the claw marks well out in front of the pad — and there were no tracks coming down. Not old ones filled in with new snow, because there had been no new snow. None at all. He stood there for a while doing the arithmetic that everybody does at that moment, which is: *if it went up, and it has not come down, then it is up there.* Then he followed the tracks, because he was twelve, and because some questions will not wait until you are older."
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "A primavera estava quatro semanas atrasada e todo mundo no vale tinha uma teoria. O senhor Varga, da oficina, dizia que era a corrente de jato, e dizia no tom de um homem que tinha aprendido havia pouco o que era uma corrente de jato. As mulheres que subiam de Brod no ônibus de quinta-feira diziam que era porque o inverno tinha sido ameno e o ano estava compensando. O padre Emil dizia que não cabia a nós saber, o que Tolek notou ser a resposta que o padre Emil dava para quase tudo, e que tinha a vantagem de estar sempre tecnicamente correta. A mãe de Tolek dizia que o tempo não devia explicação a ninguém, e mandou ele recolher a roupa do varal. Mas a amendoeira ao lado da igreja tinha soltado botões em fevereiro e depois se arrependido. Essa era a parte para a qual ninguém tinha teoria. Os botões tinham vindo, pequenos e rosados nas pontas, numa semana quente no fim do inverno. E então tinham parado. Não tinham aberto e não tinham caído. Tinham simplesmente se fechado, e endurecido, e ficado assim nos galhos por nove semanas — centenas de punhos pequenos e fechados, esperando. Árvores velhas não fazem isso. Árvores velhas se comprometem. E a neve no campo alto não estava derretendo. Também não estava caindo. Essa era a outra coisa. Não havia neve nova desde a segunda semana de março, e tinha havido sol, sol de verdade, o bastante para esquentar as pedras do muro sul. Mas a linha branca no alto da montanha não tinha descido nem um metro. Ficava onde estava, exatamente onde estava, com uma borda limpa como a de uma página. Tolek subiu até lá depois da aula numa terça-feira, com as mãos puxadas para dentro das mangas. Não estava sendo corajoso. Queria saber, e o querer tinha ficado maior que o não querer ao longo de uns catorze dias, que é como a maioria das coisas corajosas acontece de verdade. Na beira da neve ele parou e olhou para o chão. Havia pegadas subindo. Eram enormes — fundas, redondas, com as marcas das garras bem à frente da almofada — e não havia pegadas descendo. Não eram antigas e cobertas por neve nova, porque não tinha caído neve nova. Nenhuma. Ele ficou ali um tempo fazendo a aritmética que todo mundo faz nesse momento, que é: *se subiu, e não desceu, então está lá em cima.* Então seguiu as pegadas, porque tinha doze anos, e porque algumas perguntas não esperam você crescer."
          }
        }
      }
    },
