    "The attic light had been left on for as long as anyone could remember, and nobody could say who had done it. It bothered Ines more than it bothered anybody else. She had asked her uncle about it twice. The first time he had said it was cheaper than arguing about it, which was not an answer. The second time he had said that some things in a house are older than the people in it, which was less of an answer than the first one but sounded more like one. She could see it at night from the garden — a thin orange line around the edge of the trapdoor, hanging in the dark of the landing ceiling like a picture of a door rather than a door. She went up on the first warm night of the year, because the trapdoor had begun to hum. That was new. It was a low sound, not electrical, more like the noise a bee makes inside a jam jar three rooms away. It had started on the Tuesday and by the Friday she could feel it in the ladder rungs. She waited until her uncle had gone out to lock the greenhouse, and then she went up. The attic was full of moths. Not fluttering. Not knocking themselves stupid against the bulb, which is what moths do and the only thing Ines had ever seen them do. These were resting. Hundreds of them, folded flat against the rafters and the underside of the roof slates, wings closed, absolutely still, arranged the way pages are arranged in a book. They were the colour of old paper — some almost white, some the yellow of a page that has been in a window, a few nearly brown. They did not startle when her head came up through the hatch. One near her hand adjusted itself very slightly, the way a person shifts in a chair. She sat on the edge of the hatch with her legs down through it, because the floor did not look trustworthy, and she looked at the one nearest to her. There was something on its wings. Not a pattern. She knew what moth patterns looked like: eyes and speckles and the little false faces. This was a picture. A very small one, painted in the way a stamp is printed, right across both wings so that the join ran through the middle of it. It was a kitchen. A yellow kitchen with a window over the sink and a chair pulled out from the table. She looked at the next one. A boat, on a grey sea, seen from behind. She looked at the next one, and it was a face she almost knew, and then did not, and the almost stayed with her all the way down the ladder.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "ar": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "كان ضوء العلّيّة مُشعَلًا منذ ما لا يذكر أحد بدايته، ولم يستطع أحد أن يقول من أشعله. كان يزعج إينيس أكثر مما يزعج أيّ أحد آخر. سألت عمّها عنه مرتين. في المرة الأولى قال إنّ إبقاءه مشعلًا أرخص من الجدال فيه، وهذا ليس جوابًا. وفي المرة الثانية قال إنّ بعض الأشياء في البيت أقدم من الناس الذين فيه، وهذا جواب أقلّ من الأول، لكنه يشبه الجواب أكثر. كانت تراه ليلًا من الحديقة — خطًّا برتقاليًّا رفيعًا حول حافّة الباب السقفيّ، معلّقًا في عتمة سقف البسطة كصورة باب لا كباب. صعدت في أول ليلة دافئة من السنة، لأن الباب السقفيّ كان قد بدأ يطنّ. كان ذلك جديدًا. صوت منخفض، ليس كهربائيًّا، أقرب إلى الصوت الذي تصدره نحلة داخل برطمان مربّى على بُعد ثلاث غرف. بدأ يوم الثلاثاء، وبحلول الجمعة صارت تحسّه في درجات السلّم. انتظرت حتى خرج عمّها ليقفل البيت الزجاجيّ، ثم صعدت. كانت العلّيّة مملوءة بالفراشات الليلية. لا ترفرف. ولا تضرب نفسها بغباء في المصباح، وهو ما تفعله الفراشات الليلية والشيء الوحيد الذي رأت إينيس واحدة منها تفعله. هذه كانت مستريحة. مئات منها، مطويّة مسطّحة على العوارض وعلى الوجه السفليّ لبلاطات السقف، أجنحتها مغلقة، ساكنة سكونًا تامًّا، مرتّبة كما تُرتَّب الصفحات في كتاب. كانت بلون الورق القديم — بعضها أبيض تقريبًا، وبعضها بصفرة صفحة تُركت في نافذة، وقليل منها بنّيّ تقريبًا. لم تفزع حين طلع رأسها من الفتحة. واحدة قرب يدها عدّلت وضعها تعديلًا طفيفًا جدًّا، كما يتحرّك المرء في كرسيّه. جلست على حافّة الفتحة وساقاها متدلّيتان فيها، لأن الأرضيّة لم تبدُ جديرة بالثقة، ونظرت إلى أقربها إليها. كان على جناحيها شيء. ليس نقشًا. كانت تعرف كيف تبدو نقوش الفراشات: عيون وبقع ووجوه كاذبة صغيرة. هذا كان صورة. صورة صغيرة جدًّا، مرسومة كما يُطبع طابع بريد، ممتدّة عبر الجناحين معًا حتى إنّ الوصلة بينهما تمرّ في منتصفها. كانت مطبخًا. مطبخًا أصفر فيه نافذة فوق الحوض وكرسيّ مسحوب بعيدًا عن الطاولة. نظرت إلى التالية. قارب، على بحر رماديّ، مرئيّ من الخلف. نظرت إلى التالية، فإذا هي وجه تكاد تعرفه، ثم لا تعرفه، وبقي هذا القرب من المعرفة معها طوال نزولها على السلّم."
          }
        },
        "de": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Das Licht auf dem Dachboden brannte, solange irgendjemand denken konnte, und niemand konnte sagen, wer es angemacht hatte. Ines störte es mehr als alle anderen. Sie hatte ihren Onkel zweimal danach gefragt. Beim ersten Mal hatte er gesagt, das sei billiger, als darüber zu streiten, was keine Antwort war. Beim zweiten Mal hatte er gesagt, manche Dinge in einem Haus seien älter als die Leute darin, was noch weniger eine Antwort war als die erste, aber mehr nach einer klang. Nachts konnte sie es vom Garten aus sehen — eine dünne orange Linie rund um die Luke, die im Dunkel der Flurdecke hing wie das Bild einer Tür und nicht wie eine Tür. Sie stieg in der ersten warmen Nacht des Jahres hinauf, weil die Luke angefangen hatte zu summen. Das war neu. Es war ein tiefer Ton, nicht elektrisch, eher wie das Geräusch, das eine Biene in einem Marmeladenglas drei Zimmer weiter macht. Es hatte am Dienstag angefangen, und am Freitag spürte sie es in den Leitersprossen. Sie wartete, bis ihr Onkel hinausgegangen war, um das Gewächshaus abzuschließen, und dann stieg sie hinauf. Der Dachboden war voller Motten. Sie flatterten nicht. Sie schlugen sich nicht dumm gegen die Glühbirne, was Motten tun und das Einzige war, was Ines sie je hatte tun sehen. Diese ruhten. Hunderte, flach an die Dachsparren und die Unterseite der Schieferplatten gelegt, die Flügel geschlossen, vollkommen still, angeordnet, wie Seiten in einem Buch angeordnet sind. Sie hatten die Farbe von altem Papier — manche fast weiß, manche im Gelb einer Seite, die im Fenster gelegen hat, einige wenige fast braun. Sie erschraken nicht, als ihr Kopf durch die Luke kam. Eine nahe ihrer Hand richtete sich ein wenig zurecht, wie ein Mensch, der auf einem Stuhl die Haltung wechselt. Sie setzte sich auf den Rand der Luke, die Beine hindurch nach unten, weil der Boden nicht vertrauenswürdig aussah, und sah die nächste an. Auf ihren Flügeln war etwas. Kein Muster. Sie wusste, wie Mottenmuster aussehen: Augen und Sprenkel und die kleinen falschen Gesichter. Das hier war ein Bild. Ein sehr kleines, gemalt, wie eine Briefmarke gedruckt wird, quer über beide Flügel, sodass die Naht mitten hindurchlief. Es war eine Küche. Eine gelbe Küche mit einem Fenster über der Spüle und einem vom Tisch weggezogenen Stuhl. Sie sah die nächste an. Ein Boot auf grauer See, von hinten gesehen. Sie sah die nächste an, und es war ein Gesicht, das sie fast kannte, und dann doch nicht, und das Fast blieb die ganze Leiter hinunter bei ihr."
          }
        },
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The attic light had been left on for as long as anyone could remember, and nobody could say who had done it. It bothered Ines more than it bothered anybody else. She had asked her uncle about it twice. The first time he had said it was cheaper than arguing about it, which was not an answer. The second time he had said that some things in a house are older than the people in it, which was less of an answer than the first one but sounded more like one. She could see it at night from the garden — a thin orange line around the edge of the trapdoor, hanging in the dark of the landing ceiling like a picture of a door rather than a door. She went up on the first warm night of the year, because the trapdoor had begun to hum. That was new. It was a low sound, not electrical, more like the noise a bee makes inside a jam jar three rooms away. It had started on the Tuesday and by the Friday she could feel it in the ladder rungs. She waited until her uncle had gone out to lock the greenhouse, and then she went up. The attic was full of moths. Not fluttering. Not knocking themselves stupid against the bulb, which is what moths do and the only thing Ines had ever seen them do. These were resting. Hundreds of them, folded flat against the rafters and the underside of the roof slates, wings closed, absolutely still, arranged the way pages are arranged in a book. They were the colour of old paper — some almost white, some the yellow of a page that has been in a window, a few nearly brown. They did not startle when her head came up through the hatch. One near her hand adjusted itself very slightly, the way a person shifts in a chair. She sat on the edge of the hatch with her legs down through it, because the floor did not look trustworthy, and she looked at the one nearest to her. There was something on its wings. Not a pattern. She knew what moth patterns looked like: eyes and speckles and the little false faces. This was a picture. A very small one, painted in the way a stamp is printed, right across both wings so that the join ran through the middle of it. It was a kitchen. A yellow kitchen with a window over the sink and a chair pulled out from the table. She looked at the next one. A boat, on a grey sea, seen from behind. She looked at the next one, and it was a face she almost knew, and then did not, and the almost stayed with her all the way down the ladder."
          }
        },
        "es": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "La luz del desván llevaba encendida desde que nadie recordaba, y nadie sabía decir quién la había encendido. A Ines le molestaba más que a nadie. Se lo había preguntado a su tío dos veces. La primera él le dijo que salía más barato que discutirlo, lo cual no era una respuesta. La segunda dijo que algunas cosas en una casa son más viejas que la gente que vive en ella, lo cual era menos respuesta que la primera pero sonaba más a una. Se veía de noche desde el jardín: una línea naranja fina alrededor del borde de la trampilla, colgada en la oscuridad del techo del descansillo como el dibujo de una puerta más que como una puerta. Subió la primera noche cálida del año, porque la trampilla había empezado a zumbar. Eso era nuevo. Era un sonido grave, no eléctrico, más parecido al ruido que hace una abeja dentro de un tarro de mermelada a tres habitaciones de distancia. Había empezado el martes y para el viernes lo notaba en los peldaños de la escalera. Esperó a que su tío saliera a cerrar el invernadero, y subió. El desván estaba lleno de polillas. No revoloteando. No dándose de bruces contra la bombilla, que es lo que hacen las polillas y lo único que Ines había visto hacer a una. Estas descansaban. Cientos de ellas, plegadas planas contra las vigas y la cara interior de las tejas, alas cerradas, absolutamente quietas, dispuestas como se disponen las páginas en un libro. Eran del color del papel viejo: algunas casi blancas, algunas del amarillo de una página que ha estado en una ventana, unas pocas casi marrones. No se asustaron cuando la cabeza de Ines asomó por la trampilla. Una cerca de su mano se acomodó muy levemente, como se remueve una persona en una silla. Se sentó en el borde de la trampilla con las piernas colgando, porque el suelo no parecía de fiar, y miró a la más cercana. Había algo en sus alas. No un dibujo. Ella sabía cómo son los dibujos de las polillas: ojos y motas y las pequeñas caras falsas. Aquello era un cuadro. Uno muy pequeño, pintado como se imprime un sello, cruzando las dos alas de modo que la juntura pasaba por el medio. Era una cocina. Una cocina amarilla con una ventana sobre el fregadero y una silla apartada de la mesa. Miró a la siguiente. Un barco, en un mar gris, visto desde atrás. Miró a la siguiente, y era una cara que casi conocía, y luego no, y el casi se quedó con ella toda la escalera abajo."
          }
        },
        "fr": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "La lumière du grenier était restée allumée d'aussi loin qu'on s'en souvienne, et personne ne pouvait dire qui l'avait allumée. Cela dérangeait Ines plus que cela ne dérangeait qui que ce soit d'autre. Elle avait posé la question à son oncle deux fois. La première fois, il avait dit que cela coûtait moins cher que d'en discuter, ce qui n'était pas une réponse. La deuxième fois, il avait dit que certaines choses dans une maison sont plus vieilles que les gens qui l'habitent, ce qui était encore moins une réponse que la première mais y ressemblait davantage. Elle la voyait la nuit depuis le jardin — un mince trait orange autour de la trappe, suspendu dans le noir du plafond du palier comme l'image d'une porte plutôt que comme une porte. Elle monta la première nuit douce de l'année, parce que la trappe s'était mise à bourdonner. C'était nouveau. C'était un son grave, pas électrique, plutôt le bruit que fait une abeille dans un bocal à confiture à trois pièces de là. Cela avait commencé le mardi et le vendredi elle le sentait dans les barreaux de l'échelle. Elle attendit que son oncle soit sorti fermer la serre, et elle monta. Le grenier était plein de papillons de nuit. Pas en train de voleter. Pas en train de s'assommer contre l'ampoule, ce que font les papillons de nuit et la seule chose qu'Ines les eût jamais vus faire. Ceux-là se reposaient. Des centaines, repliés à plat contre les chevrons et le dessous des ardoises, ailes fermées, absolument immobiles, rangés comme les pages sont rangées dans un livre. Ils avaient la couleur du vieux papier — certains presque blancs, certains du jaune d'une page restée dans une fenêtre, quelques-uns presque bruns. Ils ne sursautèrent pas quand sa tête émergea de la trappe. L'un d'eux, près de sa main, se rajusta très légèrement, comme une personne qui change de position sur une chaise. Elle s'assit au bord de la trappe, les jambes pendantes, parce que le plancher n'inspirait pas confiance, et elle regarda celui qui était le plus près d'elle. Il y avait quelque chose sur ses ailes. Pas un motif. Elle savait à quoi ressemblent les motifs des papillons de nuit : des yeux, des mouchetures et les petits faux visages. Ceci était une image. Une très petite, peinte comme un timbre est imprimé, en travers des deux ailes, si bien que la jointure passait par le milieu. C'était une cuisine. Une cuisine jaune avec une fenêtre au-dessus de l'évier et une chaise écartée de la table. Elle regarda le suivant. Un bateau, sur une mer grise, vu de l'arrière. Elle regarda le suivant, et c'était un visage qu'elle connaissait presque, et puis non, et ce presque resta avec elle tout le long de la descente."
          }
        },
        "it": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "La luce della soffitta era rimasta accesa da quando chiunque potesse ricordare, e nessuno sapeva dire chi l'avesse lasciata così. Dava fastidio a Ines più che a chiunque altro. Lo aveva chiesto due volte a suo zio. La prima volta lui aveva detto che costava meno che discuterne, il che non era una risposta. La seconda volta aveva detto che certe cose in una casa sono più vecchie delle persone che ci stanno dentro, il che era una risposta ancora meno della prima ma somigliava di più a una. Di notte la vedeva dal giardino: una sottile linea arancione tutt'intorno al bordo della botola, sospesa nel buio del soffitto del pianerottolo come il disegno di una porta più che come una porta. Salì la prima notte calda dell'anno, perché la botola aveva cominciato a ronzare. Quello era nuovo. Era un suono basso, non elettrico, più simile al rumore che fa un'ape dentro un barattolo di marmellata a tre stanze di distanza. Era cominciato il martedì e per il venerdì lo sentiva nei pioli della scala. Aspettò che suo zio fosse uscito a chiudere la serra, e poi salì. La soffitta era piena di falene. Non svolazzanti. Non a stordirsi contro la lampadina, che è quello che fanno le falene e l'unica cosa che Ines le avesse mai viste fare. Queste riposavano. Centinaia, ripiegate piatte contro le travi e contro il rovescio delle ardesie del tetto, ali chiuse, assolutamente immobili, disposte come sono disposte le pagine in un libro. Erano del colore della carta vecchia: alcune quasi bianche, alcune del giallo di una pagina rimasta a una finestra, qualcuna quasi marrone. Non si spaventarono quando la sua testa spuntò dalla botola. Una vicino alla sua mano si sistemò appena, come una persona che cambia posizione su una sedia. Si sedette sul bordo della botola con le gambe giù attraverso l'apertura, perché il pavimento non sembrava affidabile, e guardò quella più vicina a lei. C'era qualcosa sulle sue ali. Non un motivo. Sapeva com'erano fatti i motivi delle falene: occhi e macchioline e le piccole facce finte. Questa era un'immagine. Piccolissima, dipinta nel modo in cui viene stampato un francobollo, distesa su entrambe le ali in modo che la giuntura le passasse nel mezzo. Era una cucina. Una cucina gialla con una finestra sopra il lavello e una sedia scostata dal tavolo. Guardò quella dopo. Una barca, su un mare grigio, vista da dietro. Guardò quella dopo ancora, ed era un viso che quasi conosceva, e poi no, e quel quasi le rimase addosso per tutta la discesa lungo la scala."
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "A luz do sótão estava acesa desde que qualquer um conseguia lembrar, e ninguém sabia dizer quem tinha acendido. Incomodava Ines mais do que incomodava qualquer outra pessoa. Ela tinha perguntado ao tio duas vezes. Na primeira ele tinha dito que era mais barato do que discutir sobre isso, o que não era uma resposta. Na segunda tinha dito que algumas coisas numa casa são mais velhas que as pessoas dentro dela, o que era menos resposta ainda que a primeira mas soava mais como uma. Dava para ver de noite do jardim — uma linha laranja fina em volta da borda do alçapão, pendurada no escuro do teto do patamar como o desenho de uma porta em vez de uma porta. Ela subiu na primeira noite quente do ano, porque o alçapão tinha começado a zumbir. Isso era novo. Era um som grave, não elétrico, mais parecido com o barulho que uma abelha faz dentro de um pote de geleia a três cômodos de distância. Tinha começado na terça e na sexta ela já sentia nos degraus da escada. Esperou o tio sair para trancar a estufa, e então subiu. O sótão estava cheio de mariposas. Não esvoaçando. Não se atirando feito bobas contra a lâmpada, que é o que mariposas fazem e a única coisa que Ines já tinha visto uma fazer. Estas estavam descansando. Centenas delas, dobradas rentes às vigas e à parte de baixo das telhas, asas fechadas, absolutamente imóveis, arranjadas do jeito que as páginas se arranjam num livro. Eram da cor de papel velho — algumas quase brancas, algumas do amarelo de uma página que ficou na janela, umas poucas quase marrons. Não se assustaram quando a cabeça dela apareceu pelo alçapão. Uma perto da mão dela se ajeitou muito de leve, do jeito que uma pessoa se remexe na cadeira. Ela sentou na beira do alçapão com as pernas para dentro, porque o assoalho não parecia confiável, e olhou para a mais próxima. Havia alguma coisa nas asas. Não um padrão. Ela sabia como são os padrões de mariposa: olhos e pintas e as carinhas falsas. Aquilo era um quadro. Um bem pequeno, pintado do jeito que um selo é impresso, atravessando as duas asas de modo que a emenda passava pelo meio dele. Era uma cozinha. Uma cozinha amarela com uma janela sobre a pia e uma cadeira puxada para fora da mesa. Olhou para a seguinte. Um barco, num mar cinzento, visto por trás. Olhou para a seguinte, e era um rosto que ela quase conhecia, e depois não, e o quase ficou com ela pela escada inteira até embaixo."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The bridge fell in 1998. For one night in June you can still see where it was.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "ar": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "سقط الجسر عام 1998. في ليلة واحدة من يونيو لا يزال بإمكانك رؤية موضعه."
          }
        },
        "de": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Die Brücke stürzte 1998 ein. Eine Nacht im Juni sieht man noch, wo sie stand."
          }
        },
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The bridge fell in 1998. For one night in June you can still see where it was."
          }
        },
        "es": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "El puente cayó en 1998. Una noche de junio aún puedes ver dónde estaba."
          }
        },
        "fr": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Le pont est tombé en 1998. Une nuit de juin, on voit encore où il était."
          }
        },
        "it": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Il ponte è caduto nel 1998. Per una notte di giugno si vede ancora dov’era."
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "A ponte caiu em 1998. Por uma noite de junho ainda dá para ver onde ficava."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The engineers want to move the wall. Somebody ought to ask her first.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "ar": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "يريد المهندسون نقل الجدار. ينبغي لأحد أن يسألها أولًا."
          }
        },
        "de": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Die Ingenieure wollen die Mauer versetzen. Jemand sollte sie erst fragen."
          }
        },
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The engineers want to move the wall. Somebody ought to ask her first."
          }
        },
        "es": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Los ingenieros quieren mover el muro. Alguien debería preguntarle primero."
          }
        },
        "fr": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Les ingénieurs veulent déplacer le mur. Quelqu’un devrait lui demander d’abord."
          }
        },
        "it": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Gli ingegneri vogliono spostare il muro. Qualcuno dovrebbe chiederle prima."
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Os engenheiros querem mover o muro. Alguém devia perguntar a ela antes."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The first one": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "ar": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "الأولى"
          }
        },
        "de": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Die erste"
          }
        },
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The first one"
          }
        },
        "es": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "La primera"
          }
        },
        "fr": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "La première"
          }
        },
        "it": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "La prima"
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "A primeira"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The following winter the light outside number forty was still out and there was a new reference number. Bruno was twelve by then and had stopped thinking about the sixty metres entirely, and the reason he remembered any of this at all is a girl called Cátia who moved into the flats above the pharmacy in October and who was nine. He noticed her because she stopped. He came round the bend one evening at about half five and there was a small person standing at the top of the dark part with her bag held against her front in both arms, doing the private business of deciding, and not deciding. He knew exactly what she was doing. He had done it for four months. He did not say anything about it, because he was twelve and knew that would be the wrong thing. He walked past her and said \"Boa noite\" and kept going at a normal speed, and she came after him, about four metres back, all the way down and round the corner into the light. She did that every evening for about three weeks, at four metres, and neither of them ever mentioned it. And then one evening at the start of December he was late — he had stayed for the football and come on the later bus — and he came round the bend at nearly seven, and the dark bit was properly dark, and there was nobody in it at all. And at the far end, at the corner where the light came back, something grey was sitting facing him. Bruno stopped. He stood there at the top of Rua da Cordoaria for a while, a good deal longer than he needed to, looking down sixty metres of dark at a cat he had not seen for a year and a half. Then he walked. It stood up when he was halfway, unhurried, and went round the corner ahead of him at the pace of something that assumes you are coming, and when he came into the light it was sitting ten metres on. He did not put his hand out this time. He had learned that much. He said, \"I know. I'm going.\" And it looked at him, and stood up, and walked off along the wall and was gone between two parked cars. He told his grandmother that night, because she was still the only adult he told, and she was doing the same thing at the same sink. \"It came back,\" he said. \"I'm twelve.\" \"Mm,\" his grandmother said. \"But I wasn't frightened. I was late, that's all. I was just late.\" His grandmother turned the tap off and dried her hands and looked at him for a moment with an expression he could not read at all. \"Bruno,\" she said. \"You were on your own, in the dark, and you're twelve, and you stood at the top of that street for a full minute before you moved. I watched you from the window.\" She hung the cloth on the rail. \"It isn't the cat that changes.\"": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "ar": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "في الشتاء التالي كان المصباح أمام البيت رقم أربعين ما يزال مطفأً وكان هناك رقم مرجعيّ جديد. كان برونو قد صار في الثانية عشرة وكفّ عن التفكير في الستين مترًا تمامًا، والسبب في أنه يذكر شيئًا من هذا كلّه فتاة تُدعى كاتيا انتقلت إلى الشقق فوق الصيدلية في أكتوبر وكانت في التاسعة. لاحظها لأنها توقّفت. التفّ عند المنحنى ذات مساء نحو الخامسة والنصف فإذا شخص صغير واقف في أعلى الجزء المظلم وحقيبتها مضمومة إلى صدرها بذراعيها الاثنتين، تؤدّي ذلك الشأن الخاصّ: أن تقرّر، ولا تقرّر. كان يعرف تمامًا ما تفعله. فقد فعله أربعة أشهر. ولم يقل شيئًا عن ذلك، لأنه كان في الثانية عشرة ويعرف أنّ ذلك سيكون الشيء الخطأ. مرّ بجانبها وقال «Boa noite» ومضى بسرعة عادية، فجاءت خلفه، على بُعد أربعة أمتار تقريبًا، الطريق كلّه نزولًا وحول الزاوية إلى الضوء. فعلت ذلك كلّ مساء نحو ثلاثة أسابيع، على أربعة أمتار، ولم يذكر أيّ منهما الأمر قطّ. ثم ذات مساء في أول ديسمبر تأخّر — كان قد بقي لأجل كرة القدم وجاء بالحافلة المتأخّرة — والتفّ عند المنحنى قرابة السابعة، وكان الجزء المظلم مظلمًا حقًّا، ولم يكن فيه أحد البتّة. وفي الطرف البعيد، عند الزاوية حيث يعود الضوء، كان شيء رماديّ جالسًا يواجهه. توقّف برونو. وقف هناك في أعلى Rua da Cordoaria بُرهة، أطول كثيرًا مما كان يحتاج، ينظر عبر ستين مترًا من الظلام إلى قطّ لم يره منذ سنة ونصف. ثم مشى. وقف القطّ حين بلغ منتصف الطريق، غير متعجّل، ومضى حول الزاوية أمامه على خطى شيء يفترض أنك قادم، ولمّا دخل إلى الضوء كان جالسًا على بُعد عشرة أمتار. لم يمدّ يده هذه المرة. كان قد تعلّم ذلك القدر على الأقلّ. قال: «أعرف. أنا ذاهب.» فنظر إليه، ووقف، ومشى مبتعدًا بمحاذاة الجدار واختفى بين سيارتين متوقّفتين. أخبر جدّته تلك الليلة، لأنها كانت ما تزال البالغة الوحيدة التي يخبرها، وكانت تفعل الشيء نفسه عند الحوض نفسه. «عاد» قال. «أنا في الثانية عشرة.» «مم» قالت جدّته. «لكنني لم أكن خائفًا. كنت متأخّرًا، هذا كلّ شيء. كنت متأخّرًا فقط.» أغلقت جدّته الصنبور وجفّفت يديها ونظرت إليه لحظة بتعبير لم يستطع أن يقرأه البتّة. «برونو» قالت. «كنت وحدك، في الظلام، وأنت في الثانية عشرة، ووقفت في أعلى ذلك الشارع دقيقة كاملة قبل أن تتحرّك. رأيتك من النافذة.» علّقت الفوطة على القضيب. «ليس القطّ هو الذي يتغيّر.»"
          }
        },
        "de": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Im folgenden Winter war die Laterne vor Nummer vierzig immer noch aus, und es gab ein neues Aktenzeichen. Bruno war inzwischen zwölf und dachte überhaupt nicht mehr über die sechzig Meter nach, und der Grund, warum er sich an all das überhaupt erinnert, ist ein Mädchen namens Cátia, das im Oktober in die Wohnungen über der Apotheke zog und neun war. Ihm fiel sie auf, weil sie stehen blieb. Er kam eines Abends gegen halb sechs um die Kurve, und da stand ein kleiner Mensch oben am dunklen Teil, die Tasche mit beiden Armen vor sich gedrückt, und erledigte die private Angelegenheit des Sichentscheidens und entschied sich nicht. Er wusste genau, was sie tat. Er hatte es vier Monate lang getan. Er sagte nichts dazu, weil er zwölf war und wusste, dass das das Falsche wäre. Er ging an ihr vorbei und sagte „Boa noite“ und ging in normalem Tempo weiter, und sie kam hinter ihm her, etwa vier Meter zurück, den ganzen Weg hinunter und um die Ecke ins Licht. Das tat sie ungefähr drei Wochen lang jeden Abend, auf vier Metern, und keiner von beiden erwähnte es je. Und dann war er eines Abends Anfang Dezember spät dran — er war zum Fußball geblieben und mit dem späteren Bus gekommen — und er kam kurz vor sieben um die Kurve, und das dunkle Stück war richtig dunkel, und es war überhaupt niemand darin. Und am anderen Ende, an der Ecke, wo das Licht wieder anfing, saß etwas Graues und sah ihn an. Bruno blieb stehen. Er stand eine Weile oben an der Rua da Cordoaria, ein gutes Stück länger als nötig, und sah sechzig Meter Dunkelheit hinunter auf eine Katze, die er anderthalb Jahre nicht gesehen hatte. Dann ging er. Sie stand auf, als er auf halbem Weg war, ohne Eile, und ging vor ihm um die Ecke, im Tempo von etwas, das annimmt, dass man mitkommt, und als er ins Licht kam, saß sie zehn Meter weiter. Diesmal streckte er die Hand nicht aus. So viel hatte er gelernt. Er sagte: „Ich weiß. Ich gehe ja.“ Und sie sah ihn an und stand auf und ging an der Mauer entlang davon und war zwischen zwei geparkten Autos verschwunden. Er erzählte es an jenem Abend seiner Großmutter, weil sie immer noch die einzige Erwachsene war, der er es erzählte, und sie tat dasselbe am selben Spülbecken. „Sie ist wiedergekommen“, sagte er. „Ich bin zwölf.“ „Mhm“, sagte seine Großmutter. „Aber ich hatte keine Angst. Ich war spät dran, das ist alles. Ich war nur spät dran.“ Seine Großmutter drehte den Hahn zu und trocknete sich die Hände und sah ihn einen Moment lang mit einem Ausdruck an, den er überhaupt nicht deuten konnte. „Bruno“, sagte sie. „Du warst allein, im Dunkeln, und du bist zwölf, und du hast eine ganze Minute oben an dieser Straße gestanden, bevor du dich gerührt hast. Ich habe dich vom Fenster aus gesehen.“ Sie hängte das Tuch an die Stange. „Nicht die Katze ist es, die sich ändert.“"
          }
        },
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The following winter the light outside number forty was still out and there was a new reference number. Bruno was twelve by then and had stopped thinking about the sixty metres entirely, and the reason he remembered any of this at all is a girl called Cátia who moved into the flats above the pharmacy in October and who was nine. He noticed her because she stopped. He came round the bend one evening at about half five and there was a small person standing at the top of the dark part with her bag held against her front in both arms, doing the private business of deciding, and not deciding. He knew exactly what she was doing. He had done it for four months. He did not say anything about it, because he was twelve and knew that would be the wrong thing. He walked past her and said \"Boa noite\" and kept going at a normal speed, and she came after him, about four metres back, all the way down and round the corner into the light. She did that every evening for about three weeks, at four metres, and neither of them ever mentioned it. And then one evening at the start of December he was late — he had stayed for the football and come on the later bus — and he came round the bend at nearly seven, and the dark bit was properly dark, and there was nobody in it at all. And at the far end, at the corner where the light came back, something grey was sitting facing him. Bruno stopped. He stood there at the top of Rua da Cordoaria for a while, a good deal longer than he needed to, looking down sixty metres of dark at a cat he had not seen for a year and a half. Then he walked. It stood up when he was halfway, unhurried, and went round the corner ahead of him at the pace of something that assumes you are coming, and when he came into the light it was sitting ten metres on. He did not put his hand out this time. He had learned that much. He said, \"I know. I'm going.\" And it looked at him, and stood up, and walked off along the wall and was gone between two parked cars. He told his grandmother that night, because she was still the only adult he told, and she was doing the same thing at the same sink. \"It came back,\" he said. \"I'm twelve.\" \"Mm,\" his grandmother said. \"But I wasn't frightened. I was late, that's all. I was just late.\" His grandmother turned the tap off and dried her hands and looked at him for a moment with an expression he could not read at all. \"Bruno,\" she said. \"You were on your own, in the dark, and you're twelve, and you stood at the top of that street for a full minute before you moved. I watched you from the window.\" She hung the cloth on the rail. \"It isn't the cat that changes.\""
          }
        },
        "es": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "El invierno siguiente la luz de delante del número cuarenta seguía apagada y había un número de expediente nuevo. Bruno tenía doce años para entonces y había dejado de pensar en los sesenta metros por completo, y la razón por la que recuerda algo de todo esto es una niña llamada Cátia que se mudó a los pisos de encima de la farmacia en octubre y que tenía nueve años. Se fijó en ella porque se paró. Dobló la curva una tarde a eso de las cinco y media y había una persona pequeña de pie en lo alto de la parte oscura con la mochila apretada contra el pecho con los dos brazos, haciendo el asunto privado de decidir, y no decidiendo. Él sabía exactamente lo que estaba haciendo. Lo había hecho durante cuatro meses. No dijo nada al respecto, porque tenía doce años y sabía que sería lo incorrecto. Pasó a su lado y dijo «boa noite» y siguió a velocidad normal, y ella fue detrás de él, unos cuatro metros atrás, todo el camino hasta doblar la esquina hacia la luz. Lo hizo todas las tardes durante unas tres semanas, a cuatro metros, y ninguno de los dos lo mencionó nunca. Y entonces una tarde a principios de diciembre él llegaba tarde —se había quedado al fútbol y había cogido el autobús siguiente— y dobló la curva casi a las siete, y la parte oscura estaba oscura de verdad, y no había nadie en ella. Y al fondo, en la esquina donde volvía la luz, algo gris estaba sentado mirándolo. Bruno se paró. Se quedó allí en lo alto de la Rua da Cordoaria un rato, bastante más del que necesitaba, mirando sesenta metros de oscuridad hacia un gato que no veía desde hacía año y medio. Entonces anduvo. Se levantó cuando él estaba a la mitad, sin prisa, y dobló la esquina por delante al paso de algo que da por hecho que vas detrás, y cuando él entró en la luz estaba sentado diez metros más allá. Esta vez no alargó la mano. Había aprendido eso al menos. Dijo: —Ya lo sé. Ya voy. Y lo miró, y se levantó, y se fue pegado al muro y desapareció entre dos coches aparcados. Se lo contó a su abuela aquella noche, porque seguía siendo la única adulta a la que se lo contaba, y ella estaba haciendo lo mismo en el mismo fregadero. —Ha vuelto —dijo—. Tengo doce años. —Mm —dijo su abuela. —Pero no tenía miedo. Llegaba tarde, eso es todo. Solo llegaba tarde. Su abuela cerró el grifo y se secó las manos y lo miró un momento con una expresión que él no supo leer en absoluto. —Bruno —dijo—. Estabas solo, a oscuras, y tienes doce años, y te quedaste en lo alto de esa calle un minuto entero antes de moverte. Te vi desde la ventana. —Colgó el paño en la barra—. No es el gato el que cambia."
          }
        },
        "fr": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "L'hiver suivant, le réverbère devant le numéro quarante était toujours éteint et il y avait un nouveau numéro de dossier. Bruno avait douze ans alors et avait complètement cessé de penser aux soixante mètres, et s'il se souvient de tout cela, c'est à cause d'une fille appelée Cátia qui emménagea dans les appartements au-dessus de la pharmacie en octobre et qui avait neuf ans. Il la remarqua parce qu'elle s'arrêta. Il passa le virage un soir vers cinq heures et demie et il y avait une petite personne debout en haut de la partie noire, son sac serré contre elle à deux bras, en train de faire l'affaire privée qui consiste à décider, et ne décidant pas. Il savait exactement ce qu'elle faisait. Il l'avait fait pendant quatre mois. Il n'en dit rien, parce qu'il avait douze ans et savait que cela aurait été la mauvaise chose à faire. Il passa devant elle et dit « Boa noite » et continua à vitesse normale, et elle le suivit, à quatre mètres derrière, tout le long et jusqu'au coin dans la lumière. Elle fit cela tous les soirs pendant environ trois semaines, à quatre mètres, et ni l'un ni l'autre n'en parla jamais. Et puis, un soir au début de décembre, il fut en retard — il était resté pour le football et avait pris le bus suivant — et il passa le virage à près de sept heures, et la partie noire était franchement noire, et il n'y avait absolument personne dedans. Et tout au bout, au coin où la lumière revenait, quelque chose de gris était assis, tourné vers lui. Bruno s'arrêta. Il resta là en haut de la Rua da Cordoaria un moment, bien plus longtemps qu'il n'en avait besoin, à regarder soixante mètres de noir au bout desquels se tenait un chat qu'il n'avait pas vu depuis un an et demi. Puis il marcha. Le chat se leva quand il fut à mi-chemin, sans se presser, et tourna le coin devant lui à l'allure de quelque chose qui suppose que vous venez, et quand il arriva dans la lumière le chat était assis dix mètres plus loin. Il ne tendit pas la main cette fois. Il avait appris au moins cela. Il dit : — Je sais. J'y vais. Et le chat le regarda, et se leva, et s'en alla le long du mur et disparut entre deux voitures garées. Il en parla à sa grand-mère ce soir-là, parce qu'elle était toujours le seul adulte à qui il en parlait, et elle faisait la même chose au même évier. — Il est revenu, dit-il. J'ai douze ans. — Mm, dit sa grand-mère. — Mais je n'avais pas peur. J'étais en retard, c'est tout. J'étais juste en retard. Sa grand-mère ferma le robinet et s'essuya les mains et le regarda un instant avec une expression qu'il ne put pas déchiffrer du tout. — Bruno, dit-elle. Tu étais tout seul, dans le noir, et tu as douze ans, et tu es resté en haut de cette rue une minute entière avant de bouger. Je t'ai vu de la fenêtre. Elle accrocha le torchon à la barre. — Ce n'est pas le chat qui change."
          }
        },
        "it": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "L'inverno successivo il lampione davanti al numero quaranta era ancora spento e c'era un nuovo numero di pratica. Bruno ormai aveva dodici anni e aveva smesso del tutto di pensare ai sessanta metri, e il motivo per cui si ricorda qualcosa di tutto questo è una ragazzina di nome Cátia che a ottobre si trasferì negli appartamenti sopra la farmacia e che aveva nove anni. La notò perché si fermava. Una sera girò la curva verso le cinque e mezza e c'era una personcina in piedi in cima al tratto buio con lo zaino stretto davanti con tutte e due le braccia, a sbrigare la faccenda privata di decidere, e a non decidere. Sapeva esattamente cosa stesse facendo. L'aveva fatto per quattro mesi. Non disse niente al riguardo, perché aveva dodici anni e sapeva che sarebbe stata la cosa sbagliata. Le passò accanto e disse — Boa noite — e proseguì a velocità normale, e lei gli venne dietro, a circa quattro metri, per tutta la discesa e oltre l'angolo dentro la luce. Lo fece tutte le sere per circa tre settimane, a quattro metri, e nessuno dei due ne parlò mai. E poi una sera all'inizio di dicembre fece tardi — era rimasto per il calcio ed era tornato con l'autobus successivo — e girò la curva verso le sette, e il tratto buio era buio pesto, e non c'era proprio nessuno. E in fondo, all'angolo dove la luce ritornava, qualcosa di grigio stava seduto rivolto verso di lui. Bruno si fermò. Rimase lì in cima a Rua da Cordoaria per un po', parecchio più a lungo di quanto gli servisse, a guardare lungo sessanta metri di buio un gatto che non vedeva da un anno e mezzo. Poi camminò. Il gatto si alzò quando lui era a metà, senza fretta, e girò l'angolo davanti a lui al passo di qualcosa che dà per scontato che tu stia arrivando, e quando lui entrò nella luce era seduto dieci metri più avanti. Questa volta non allungò la mano. Quello almeno l'aveva imparato. Disse: — Lo so. Sto andando. E il gatto lo guardò, e si alzò, e se ne andò lungo il muro e sparì tra due macchine parcheggiate. Lo raccontò a sua nonna quella sera, perché era ancora l'unica adulta a cui lo raccontasse, e lei stava facendo la stessa cosa allo stesso lavello. — È tornato — disse. — Ho dodici anni. — Mm — disse sua nonna. — Ma non avevo paura. Ero in ritardo, tutto qui. Ero solo in ritardo. Sua nonna chiuse il rubinetto e si asciugò le mani e lo guardò per un momento con un'espressione che lui non riuscì affatto a decifrare. — Bruno — disse. — Eri da solo, al buio, e hai dodici anni, e sei rimasto in cima a quella strada per un minuto intero prima di muoverti. Ti ho visto dalla finestra. Appese lo strofinaccio alla barra. — Non è il gatto che cambia."
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "No inverno seguinte a luz em frente ao número quarenta continuava apagada e havia um número de protocolo novo. Bruno tinha doze anos a essa altura e tinha parado de pensar nos sessenta metros por completo, e a razão de ele lembrar qualquer coisa disso é uma menina chamada Cátia que se mudou para os apartamentos acima da farmácia em outubro e que tinha nove anos. Ele reparou nela porque ela parou. Fez a curva uma noite por volta das cinco e meia e havia uma pessoa pequena parada no alto da parte escura com a mochila apertada contra o peito nos dois braços, fazendo o assunto particular de decidir, e não decidindo. Ele sabia exatamente o que ela estava fazendo. Tinha feito por quatro meses. Não disse nada sobre isso, porque tinha doze anos e sabia que seria a coisa errada. Passou por ela e disse \"boa noite\" e seguiu em velocidade normal, e ela veio atrás dele, uns quatro metros atrás, o caminho todo até dobrar a esquina para a luz. Ela fez isso toda noite por umas três semanas, a quatro metros, e nenhum dos dois jamais mencionou. E então numa noite no começo de dezembro ele estava atrasado — tinha ficado para o futebol e pegado o ônibus mais tarde — e fez a curva quase às sete, e a parte escura estava escura de verdade, e não havia ninguém nela. E na outra ponta, na esquina onde a luz voltava, alguma coisa cinza estava sentada virada para ele. Bruno parou. Ficou ali no alto da Rua da Cordoaria por um tempo, bem mais do que precisava, olhando sessenta metros de escuro abaixo para um gato que não via havia um ano e meio. Então andou. Ele levantou quando Bruno estava na metade, sem pressa, e dobrou a esquina à frente dele no passo de quem presume que você vem junto, e quando ele entrou na luz estava sentado dez metros adiante. Ele não estendeu a mão dessa vez. Tinha aprendido isso pelo menos. Disse: — Eu sei. Estou indo. E ele olhou para Bruno, e levantou, e foi embora rente ao muro e sumiu entre dois carros estacionados. Ele contou à avó naquela noite, porque ela continuava sendo a única adulta a quem ele contava, e ela estava fazendo a mesma coisa na mesma pia. — Ele voltou — disse. — Eu tenho doze anos. — Hum — disse a avó. — Mas eu não estava com medo. Eu estava atrasado, só isso. Eu só estava atrasado. A avó fechou a torneira e secou as mãos e olhou para ele por um instante com uma expressão que ele não conseguiu ler de jeito nenhum. — Bruno — disse. — Você estava sozinho, no escuro, e você tem doze anos, e você ficou parado no alto daquela rua por um minuto inteiro antes de se mexer. Eu vi da janela. — Pendurou o pano no varão. — Não é o gato que muda."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The harbour used to read them. Now there is an app, and the app was wrong on the Tuesday.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "ar": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "كان الميناء يقرؤهم. الآن هناك تطبيق، والتطبيق أخطأ يوم الثلاثاء."
          }
        },
        "de": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Der Hafen las sie früher. Jetzt gibt es eine App, und die App lag am Dienstag falsch."
          }
        },
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The harbour used to read them. Now there is an app, and the app was wrong on the Tuesday."
          }
        },
        "es": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "El puerto solía leerlos. Ahora hay una app, y la app se equivocó el martes."
          }
        },
        "fr": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Le port les lisait avant. Maintenant il y a une app, et l’app s’est trompée le mardi."
          }
        },
        "it": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Il porto li leggeva. Ora c’è un’app, e l’app ha sbagliato il martedì."
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "O porto costumava lê-los. Agora existe um aplicativo, e o aplicativo errou na terça."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The hare kept an office in the root of a beech, which Marisol found by walking into it. She had been going along the top edge of the wood where the bracken thins out, not paying attention, thinking about something else entirely, and her boot went into a gap between two roots and stopped against something that was not earth. It made a sound. Wood on wood. A small hollow knock, like knuckles on a door, which in a sense it was. She got down on her knees and put her eye to the gap. There was a room in there. It was about the size of a bread oven and it had been fitted out with considerable care. There was a desk made from a slate roof tile balanced on two flints. There was a lamp that was not a lamp but a piece of foxfire in a nutshell, giving off that cold green light that rotten wood gives off in autumn. And there were papers — a great many papers, rolled and stacked in the corners and held down against the draught with acorns. The hare was at the desk, drawing. It was an ordinary hare in every respect that Marisol could see. Brown, long, with the black tips on the ears and the eye set high in the side of the head the way a hare's eye is, so that it can see behind itself without turning. It was holding something in one forepaw and moving it across the paper in short strokes. It went on drawing for some time after she put her eye to the gap. Then it said, without looking up: \"You're standing in the light.\" Marisol moved. \"Thank you.\" She waited. It kept drawing. She was aware that she was kneeling in wet bracken and that her knees were getting cold and that she did not want to be anywhere else. Eventually the hare put the stylus down and turned the paper round with both paws so that she could see it through the gap. It was a map of her wood. She knew it immediately, and it was a good map. There was the stream, with the two kinks in it near the bottom where it goes round the boulder. There was the fallen oak that had come down in the storm three winters ago and had been lying across the bottom path ever since, forcing everyone into a detour through the nettles. There was the fence, and the place where the fence gives up and just stops, halfway along, for no reason anyone has ever explained. And there was a path. It ran from the stile at the bottom corner, up along the flank of the hill, and over the top to the ridge. It was drawn firmly, as a path is drawn, with a dashed line and a little scale bar beside it: eight hundred metres. Marisol had lived at the bottom of that wood her whole life. \"There isn't a path there,\" she said. \"No,\" the hare agreed. \"I've been here twelve years. There's no path there. There's bracken up to here and then a bank and then more bracken.\" \"Yes,\" said the hare. \"It's a matter of timing.\"": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The hare kept an office in the root of a beech, which Marisol found by walking into it. She had been going along the top edge of the wood where the bracken thins out, not paying attention, thinking about something else entirely, and her boot went into a gap between two roots and stopped against something that was not earth. It made a sound. Wood on wood. A small hollow knock, like knuckles on a door, which in a sense it was. She got down on her knees and put her eye to the gap. There was a room in there. It was about the size of a bread oven and it had been fitted out with considerable care. There was a desk made from a slate roof tile balanced on two flints. There was a lamp that was not a lamp but a piece of foxfire in a nutshell, giving off that cold green light that rotten wood gives off in autumn. And there were papers — a great many papers, rolled and stacked in the corners and held down against the draught with acorns. The hare was at the desk, drawing. It was an ordinary hare in every respect that Marisol could see. Brown, long, with the black tips on the ears and the eye set high in the side of the head the way a hare's eye is, so that it can see behind itself without turning. It was holding something in one forepaw and moving it across the paper in short strokes. It went on drawing for some time after she put her eye to the gap. Then it said, without looking up: \"You're standing in the light.\" Marisol moved. \"Thank you.\" She waited. It kept drawing. She was aware that she was kneeling in wet bracken and that her knees were getting cold and that she did not want to be anywhere else. Eventually the hare put the stylus down and turned the paper round with both paws so that she could see it through the gap. It was a map of her wood. She knew it immediately, and it was a good map. There was the stream, with the two kinks in it near the bottom where it goes round the boulder. There was the fallen oak that had come down in the storm three winters ago and had been lying across the bottom path ever since, forcing everyone into a detour through the nettles. There was the fence, and the place where the fence gives up and just stops, halfway along, for no reason anyone has ever explained. And there was a path. It ran from the stile at the bottom corner, up along the flank of the hill, and over the top to the ridge. It was drawn firmly, as a path is drawn, with a dashed line and a little scale bar beside it: eight hundred metres. Marisol had lived at the bottom of that wood her whole life. \"There isn't a path there,\" she said. \"No,\" the hare agreed. \"I've been here twelve years. There's no path there. There's bracken up to here and then a bank and then more bracken.\" \"Yes,\" said the hare. \"It's a matter of timing.\""
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "A lebre mantinha um escritório na raiz de uma faia, que Marisol encontrou ao dar de cara com ele. Ela vinha andando pela borda de cima do bosque, onde a samambaia rareia, sem prestar atenção, pensando em outra coisa completamente, e a bota entrou num vão entre duas raízes e parou contra uma coisa que não era terra. Fez um som. Madeira contra madeira. Uma batidinha oca, como nós dos dedos numa porta, o que em certo sentido era. Ela se ajoelhou e pôs o olho no vão. Havia um cômodo lá dentro. Era mais ou menos do tamanho de um forno de pão e tinha sido mobiliado com considerável cuidado. Havia uma escrivaninha feita de uma telha de ardósia equilibrada sobre duas pederneiras. Havia uma lâmpada que não era uma lâmpada e sim um pedaço de madeira fosforescente dentro de uma casca de noz, dando aquela luz verde e fria que a madeira podre dá no outono. E havia papéis — muitíssimos papéis, enrolados e empilhados nos cantos e presos contra a corrente de ar com bolotas. A lebre estava na escrivaninha, desenhando. Era uma lebre comum em todos os aspectos que Marisol conseguia ver. Marrom, comprida, com as pontas pretas nas orelhas e o olho alto na lateral da cabeça do jeito que o olho de uma lebre é, para que ela possa enxergar atrás de si sem se virar. Segurava alguma coisa com uma das patas dianteiras e a movia pelo papel em traços curtos. Continuou desenhando por um tempo depois que ela pôs o olho no vão. Então disse, sem levantar a cabeça: — Você está na minha luz. Marisol saiu do caminho. — Obrigada. Ela esperou. A lebre continuou desenhando. Tinha consciência de que estava ajoelhada na samambaia molhada e de que seus joelhos estavam ficando frios e de que não queria estar em nenhum outro lugar. Por fim a lebre pousou o estilete e virou o papel com as duas patas para que ela pudesse ver pelo vão. Era um mapa do bosque dela. Reconheceu na hora, e era um bom mapa. Lá estava o riacho, com as duas curvas perto da parte de baixo onde ele contorna o matacão. Lá estava o carvalho caído que tinha vindo abaixo na tempestade de três invernos atrás e que estava atravessado no caminho de baixo desde então, obrigando todo mundo a um desvio pelas urtigas. Lá estava a cerca, e o lugar onde a cerca desiste e simplesmente acaba, no meio do percurso, sem motivo que alguém jamais tenha explicado. E havia um caminho. Ia da escadinha da cerca no canto de baixo, subindo pelo flanco do morro, e passava por cima até a crista. Estava desenhado com firmeza, como se desenha um caminho, com uma linha tracejada e uma barrinha de escala ao lado: oitocentos metros. Marisol tinha morado no pé daquele bosque a vida inteira. — Não tem caminho nenhum ali — disse. — Não — concordou a lebre. — Estou aqui há doze anos. Não tem caminho ali. Tem samambaia até aqui e depois um barranco e depois mais samambaia. — Sim — disse a lebre. — É uma questão de tempo."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The hare's map shows a road through the wood. There is no road through the wood. Not yet.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "ar": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "خريطة الأرنب تُظهر طريقًا يعبر الغابة. لا يوجد طريق يعبر الغابة. ليس بعد."
          }
        },
        "de": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Die Karte des Hasen zeigt einen Weg durch den Wald. Es gibt keinen Weg durch den Wald. Noch nicht."
          }
        },
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The hare's map shows a road through the wood. There is no road through the wood. Not yet."
          }
        },
        "es": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "El mapa de la liebre muestra un camino por el bosque. No hay camino por el bosque. Aún no."
          }
        },
        "fr": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "La carte du lièvre montre une route à travers le bois. Il n’y a pas de route à travers le bois. Pas encore."
          }
        },
        "it": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "La mappa della lepre mostra una strada nel bosco. Non c’è strada nel bosco. Non ancora."
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "O mapa da lebre mostra um caminho pelo bosque. Não há caminho pelo bosque. Ainda não."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The kite was good. That was the frustrating part. If the kite had been bad, Dai would have known what to do. He would have taken it apart and started again and the whole thing would have been simple. But it was good, and he knew it was good, and it still would not fly. He had made it in April from his father's instructions, which were written on the back of an envelope in his father's small square handwriting and which had been in the kitchen drawer for six years. The spars were bamboo out of the old window blind that had been in the shed since before they moved in. The sail was paper the colour of weak tea, glued along the folds and then glued again where the first glue had gone brittle. The bridle knots were the ones on the envelope, tied four times each until he got them right. Every measurement was correct. He had checked them against the envelope twice and against a metre rule once. It went up eight feet and came down like a dropped coat. It did it thirteen times. Not a spin, not a dive — nothing dramatic enough to be interesting. It went up, it hesitated, the nose came round, and it settled onto the grass with a small papery sound that Dai came to hate more than he had ever hated a sound. The crows on the telephone wire watched all six. There were seven of them. He noticed the number on about the fourth attempt, because they were in a row and evenly spaced, which crows are not usually, and because they had not moved. Crows on a wire shuffle. They open a wing and shut it. They shout at each other. They leave and come back. These sat. They watched the way a builder watches somebody else's wall — not unkindly, but with a professional attention that made Dai's ears go hot. On the twelfth attempt, when the kite came down again in exactly the same way, the smallest of them made a sound. It was not a caw. It was short and flat and came out of the side of its beak, and Dai understood it immediately and completely, with no evidence whatsoever, to be a criticism of his tail. He stood in the middle of the field with the string in his hand and looked up at the wire and said, out loud, to seven birds: \"It's the length on the envelope.\" The smallest one turned its head so that one eye was pointing at him, and said nothing at all, which was somehow much worse.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The kite was good. That was the frustrating part. If the kite had been bad, Dai would have known what to do. He would have taken it apart and started again and the whole thing would have been simple. But it was good, and he knew it was good, and it still would not fly. He had made it in April from his father's instructions, which were written on the back of an envelope in his father's small square handwriting and which had been in the kitchen drawer for six years. The spars were bamboo out of the old window blind that had been in the shed since before they moved in. The sail was paper the colour of weak tea, glued along the folds and then glued again where the first glue had gone brittle. The bridle knots were the ones on the envelope, tied four times each until he got them right. Every measurement was correct. He had checked them against the envelope twice and against a metre rule once. It went up eight feet and came down like a dropped coat. It did it thirteen times. Not a spin, not a dive — nothing dramatic enough to be interesting. It went up, it hesitated, the nose came round, and it settled onto the grass with a small papery sound that Dai came to hate more than he had ever hated a sound. The crows on the telephone wire watched all six. There were seven of them. He noticed the number on about the fourth attempt, because they were in a row and evenly spaced, which crows are not usually, and because they had not moved. Crows on a wire shuffle. They open a wing and shut it. They shout at each other. They leave and come back. These sat. They watched the way a builder watches somebody else's wall — not unkindly, but with a professional attention that made Dai's ears go hot. On the twelfth attempt, when the kite came down again in exactly the same way, the smallest of them made a sound. It was not a caw. It was short and flat and came out of the side of its beak, and Dai understood it immediately and completely, with no evidence whatsoever, to be a criticism of his tail. He stood in the middle of the field with the string in his hand and looked up at the wire and said, out loud, to seven birds: \"It's the length on the envelope.\" The smallest one turned its head so that one eye was pointing at him, and said nothing at all, which was somehow much worse."
          }
        },
        "pt-BR": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "A pipa era boa. Essa era a parte frustrante. Se a pipa fosse ruim, Dai saberia o que fazer. Teria desmontado tudo e começado de novo e a coisa toda seria simples. Mas era boa, e ele sabia que era boa, e mesmo assim não voava. Tinha feito a pipa em abril a partir das instruções do pai, que estavam escritas no verso de um envelope na letra pequena e quadrada do pai e que estavam na gaveta da cozinha havia seis anos. As varetas eram de bambu da velha persiana que estava no galpão desde antes de eles se mudarem. A vela era papel da cor de chá fraco, colado ao longo das dobras e depois colado de novo onde a primeira cola tinha ressecado. Os nós do cabresto eram os do envelope, amarrados quatro vezes cada um até ele acertar. Todas as medidas estavam certas. Tinha conferido duas vezes contra o envelope e uma vez contra uma régua de um metro. Subia dois metros e meio e caía como um casaco derrubado. Fez isso treze vezes. Não era um giro, não era um mergulho — nada dramático o bastante para ser interessante. Subia, hesitava, o bico virava, e ela pousava na grama com um pequeno som de papel que Dai passou a odiar mais do que jamais tinha odiado um som. Os corvos no fio de telefone assistiram a todas as seis. Eram sete deles. Ele notou o número mais ou menos na quarta tentativa, porque estavam em fila e uniformemente espaçados, o que corvos normalmente não estão, e porque não tinham se mexido. Corvos num fio se remexem. Abrem uma asa e fecham. Gritam uns com os outros. Vão embora e voltam. Aqueles ficavam parados. Assistiam do jeito que um pedreiro assiste ao muro de outra pessoa — não com maldade, mas com uma atenção profissional que fez as orelhas de Dai esquentarem. Na décima segunda tentativa, quando a pipa caiu de novo exatamente do mesmo jeito, o menor deles fez um som. Não foi um grasnado. Foi curto e seco e saiu pelo canto do bico, e Dai entendeu na hora e por completo, sem prova nenhuma, que era uma crítica à rabiola dele. Ficou no meio do campo com a linha na mão e olhou para o fio e disse, em voz alta, para sete pássaros: — É o comprimento que está no envelope. O menor virou a cabeça de modo que um olho ficou apontado para ele, e não disse absolutamente nada, o que de algum modo foi muito pior."
          }
        }
      }
    },
